ield/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fbb179e1-20cb-58d0-b8da-a19ff4125371" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x-2y\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262076} :answer-field/id #uuid "8edf0788-7859-57f4-ac13-2b85b5bf5d5f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262376}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4b1f3451-5b4c-525b-84a2-e17f8516fc90" :question/math-academy-id "q-126547" :question/problem "Given that $F(x,y)={y}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)\\,i+({x}^{2}-y)\\,j,$ what is the scalar curl of $F?$" :question/worked-solution "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $F$ all exist, then the *scalar* curl of $F$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}-y)-\\frac{∂}{∂y}({y}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)) \\\\  & =2x-2y\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266076 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1dcb603b-689d-50ce-83f8-44f5250d8024" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)+2{y}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263275} {:answer/id #uuid "28606d0e-2a18-5568-9cc4-e5fe71ce5446" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)-2y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263349} {:answer/id #uuid "48bffaf6-9a7c-512d-a61a-b87966780f67" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263626} {:answer/id #uuid "6273b842-ef76-552a-bc8b-402fc78dd30d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263845} {:answer/id #uuid "a42cf267-cd93-5235-bac2-c5ef6a96f055" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264400}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6273b842-ef76-552a-bc8b-402fc78dd30d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263845} :answer-field/id #uuid "8a671a21-e6ba-55dc-abe6-747d123487e6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265491}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e76ba6c5-d6f9-5c6d-bdbf-7e135802e67b" :question/math-academy-id "q-131344" :question/problem "Given that $F(x,y)=({y}^{2}+{e}^{x})\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)\\,j,$ what is the scalar curl of $F?$" :question/worked-solution "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $F$ all exist, then the *scalar* curl of $F$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy))-\\frac{∂}{∂y}({y}^{2}+{e}^{x}) \\\\  & =y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)-2y \\\\  & =y(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)-2).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266078 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "094b4e33-453c-5089-9253-e8aa4183aa51" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263087} {:answer/id #uuid "2f34bbbf-e9de-56ed-b5d5-8fd90694b1a1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263397} {:answer/id #uuid "4e08ff17-d0ef-5f8f-ad8b-de16401e07eb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263677} {:answer/id #uuid "91330e43-4daf-53a5-80cb-73904b06cc42" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264246} {:answer/id #uuid "f949f546-1d67-5e4c-8955-0d191616d3a5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265181}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "094b4e33-453c-5089-9253-e8aa4183aa51" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263087} :answer-field/id #uuid "cbc7fc2f-2d80-546c-928f-f71b49a246d1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265606}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5ab1d5d9-c180-5acc-b2cc-0f6200926992" :question/math-academy-id "q-131348" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F(x,y)=({x}^{2}+xy)\\,i+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y)\\,j.$ Evaluate the scalar curl of $F$ at the point $(\\frac{1}{2},0).$" :question/worked-solution "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $F$ all exist, then the *scalar* curl of $F$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y))-\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+xy) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{x+y}-x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the scalar curl at the point $(\\frac{1}{2},0),$ and obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})}_{(1/2,0)} & =\\frac{1}{(\\frac{1}{2}+0)}-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =2-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362536 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b80ad287-ac84-5ca1-86d0-c5e6390eb3d1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6xy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362538}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b80ad287-ac84-5ca1-86d0-c5e6390eb3d1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6xy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362538} :answer-field/id #uuid "e71f8d4b-84bb-5e5f-b15b-3ca03cb3cddb" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362537}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e244d7dd-417f-51de-bb65-cf4cf4f73429" :question/math-academy-id "q-169202" :question/problem "The scalar curl of $F$ is\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ." :question/worked-solution "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $F$ all exist, then the *scalar* curl of $F$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-{y}^{3})-\\frac{∂}{∂y}(3x{y}^{2}) \\\\  & =0-(3x\\cdot 2y) \\\\  & =-6xy.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383062 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "69cdb73c-68f9-51c3-a494-dbe4266db912" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383064} {:answer/id #uuid "417e54d2-9717-5a97-a672-bbd2ccb8aafb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383065} {:answer/id #uuid "f4cc8f77-f135-588f-ab23-c3509ab33e20" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383066} {:answer/id #uuid "2f9778f5-bd6a-5d4b-81d4-8238d7ae5689" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383067} {:answer/id #uuid "6247fb45-a096-58a2-8ab7-eac0357ef0e6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383068}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f4cc8f77-f135-588f-ab23-c3509ab33e20" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383066} :answer-field/id #uuid "1329f6f4-d27c-52fa-b484-d7367ccdea8f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383063}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c88bce86-de64-567d-8976-da96551577ee" :question/math-academy-id "q-126520" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F(x,y)=(x+{y}^{2})\\,i+y\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)\\,j.$ Evaluate the scalar curl of $F$ at the point $(0,-1).$" :question/worked-solution "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $F$ all exist, then the *scalar* curl of $F$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(y\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1))-\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x+{y}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2xy}{{x}^{2}+1}-2y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the scalar curl at the point $(0,-1),$ and obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})}_{(0,-1)} & =\\frac{2(0)(-1)}{{0}^{2}+1}-2(-1) \\\\  & =0+2 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383069 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "60ac814a-bd23-5ee0-b4f1-9f60f6f92a04" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383071} {:answer/id #uuid "413bc497-4dc9-517b-955f-ff234af7121f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383072} {:answer/id #uuid "68aee1b1-ce3f-5ba3-8aee-10ef06e824b5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383073} {:answer/id #uuid "b4a78035-db24-5d4f-b02e-a5266dbde465" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383074} {:answer/id #uuid "3fb908f3-12e2-51fd-82ab-4f09ca5fad8f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383075}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "60ac814a-bd23-5ee0-b4f1-9f60f6f92a04" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383071} :answer-field/id #uuid "83fe23ea-1458-55ab-bb2d-e5ba77ca30b4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383070}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a595714d-0237-5554-9973-668e9384b87c" :question/math-academy-id "q-126527" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F(x,y)=(x-{y}^{2})\\,i+({y}^{3}-x)\\,j.$ Evaluate the scalar curl of $F$ at the point $(-1,1).$" :question/worked-solution "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $F$ all exist, then the *scalar* curl of $F$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({y}^{3}-x)-\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x-{y}^{2}) \\\\  & =-1-(-2y) \\\\  & =2y-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the scalar curl at the point $(-1,1),$ and obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})}_{(-1,1)} & =2(1)-1 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383076 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "be488c31-9256-566c-b32a-08e6c160f22c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1+2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383078} {:answer/id #uuid "f7488729-2ee4-53c3-8133-c6ecbdfd35b7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2-y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383079} {:answer/id #uuid "53b1ed95-0584-576a-b16d-faf84c6c5497" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3-\\operatorname{sin}⁡y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383080} {:answer/id #uuid "045cf962-a0e3-5f5d-9614-af24c41f8a68" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2-x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383081} {:answer/id #uuid "4063a276-ab18-5a28-835e-27da301a0497" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡y-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383082}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "045cf962-a0e3-5f5d-9614-af24c41f8a68" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2-x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383081} :answer-field/id #uuid "b22a1cc1-84ab-59a2-be59-cc57b115de95" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383077}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8cde4f56-4a8b-58dd-9183-074bcd7df016" :question/math-academy-id "q-126537" :question/problem "Given that $F(x,y)=x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,i+(2x+3y)\\,j,$ what is the scalar curl of $F?$" :question/worked-solution "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $F$ all exist, then the *scalar* curl of $F$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2x+3y)-\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y) \\\\  & =2-x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409609 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0fd5ff55-9267-592c-8f53-54d27c28ddbe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2({y}^{2}-{x}^{2})}{{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409591} {:answer/id #uuid "64ee45f8-90e8-584e-9ee0-f763b1794d3a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2xy}{{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409596} {:answer/id #uuid "81f3041d-6679-5b2e-8990-3525e4b2226c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{4xy}{{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409598} {:answer/id #uuid "9811b973-e6d5-574c-9870-5a2db507cfc5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409600} {:answer/id #uuid "d498b33f-c852-5e73-bfc6-bc624cb1045e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4xy}{{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409602}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0fd5ff55-9267-592c-8f53-54d27c28ddbe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2({y}^{2}-{x}^{2})}{{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409591} :answer-field/id #uuid "3d405633-3d03-5b83-b9d8-c7d039eaf05b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409606}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5a957b95-e78d-5791-8e30-44fc756d4a5e" :question/math-academy-id "q-131343" :question/problem "Given that $F(x,y)=\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,i+\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,j,$ what is the scalar curl of $F?$" :question/worked-solution "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $F$ all exist, then the *scalar* curl of $F$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}})-\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}}{{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})}^{2}}-\\frac{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}{{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2({y}^{2}-{x}^{2})}{{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Scalar Curl of a Vector Field"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133899 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133715 :question/id #uuid "18eef985-0fd4-5b08-8072-684ded227ab4" :question/math-academy-id "e-9557" :question/problem "![](images/16/16026d4ac7deeb30c1abb3a066df23a06a544116463b364b0a1e9e0bac0608c8.png)\n\nSuppose that $\\mathbf F$ is a vector field on $\\mathbb R^2$ defined over the region $D$ above. Given that \n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_{C_1}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\textrm d \\mathbf r = 10, \n\\qquad \n\\oint\\limits_{C_2}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\textrm d \\mathbf r = -8, \n\\qquad \n\\oint\\limits_{C_3}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\textrm d \\mathbf r = 3,\n$$\n\nwhere $C_1$ and $C_3$ are oriented clockwise, and $C_2$ is oriented counterclockwise, calculate the value of $\\displaystyle\\oint\\limits_{\\partial D}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\textrm d \\mathbf r.$" :question/worked-solution "To calculate the total circulation of a vector field over the boundary of a region containing holes, we add together the circulations over each *positively oriented* boundary.\n\nRemember that:\n\n- the positive direction is *counterclockwise* for the *outer* curve $C_1$ (because the region $D$ is always on the left as we traverse $C_1$ counterclockwise), and\n\n- the positive direction is *clockwise* for the *inner* curves $C_2$ and $C_3$ (because the region $D$ is always on the left as we traverse $C_2$ and $C_3$ clockwise).\n\nThe diagram below shows the positive orientations of all three curves.\n\n![](images/11/11445c083440ee2fde1864d599a8bbd68146d522c61de11b3e90dc231e6eddf1.png)\n\nNotice that the positive orientations of the outer curve $C_1$ and the inner curve $C_2$ are opposite those stated in the question.\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{∂D}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & =-{∮}_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r-{∮}_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{∮}_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & =-10-(-8)+3 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "56caab23-f54d-58ee-99db-99c651f78e5c" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134098 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a29028a4-0f8a-5b2f-99f1-47815863bc92" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2π" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130745} {:answer/id #uuid "cc41a40b-917a-57ad-93c5-f9db0205c2ff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130746} {:answer/id #uuid "34e14694-9434-5ad9-aa2d-2969b9ae1cc1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130747} {:answer/id #uuid "4b2ce2f4-0374-5148-947b-96f3d8b6b4ab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130748} {:answer/id #uuid "5d7d99a7-294b-5cf9-90af-ed4075071bb7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130749}] :answer-field/id #uuid "59065be7-12d4-5585-856f-70e63556d4de" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130744}] :question/id #uuid "aa43bdf8-83a8-50c7-a713-6a675dec7cf1" :question/math-academy-id "q-126558" :question/problem "![](images/72/7264984a1c19c247c030feae04615bb0c46205ca70475db1240db964ad6007b2.png)\n\nSuppose that $F$ is a vector field on $R^{2}$ defined over the region $D$ above. Given that\n\n$∮_(C_{1})F \\cdot dr = 10, ∮_(C_{2})F \\cdot dr =-4, ∮_(C_{3})F \\cdot dr =-2$,\n\nwhere $C_{1}$ is oriented counterclockwise and $C_{2}$ and $C_{3}$ are oriented clockwise, calculate the value of $∮_(∂D)F \\cdot dr$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134102 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f8dac37b-0c3a-5985-b30b-b455cf17748a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130769} {:answer/id #uuid "0f82c5b5-9ec9-5493-9c5e-088d8f3b2063" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130770} {:answer/id #uuid "226dc38b-e879-5d2e-a506-237fa0f69c69" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130771} {:answer/id #uuid "6b90ef5b-f492-5c0a-b77c-3339c17e5782" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130772} {:answer/id #uuid "6c604190-84cc-5e3c-a8c7-3b023549d9b4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130773}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "226dc38b-e879-5d2e-a506-237fa0f69c69" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130771} :answer-field/id #uuid "031169e4-3e81-5b19-b1f4-b5af34d5e90a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130768}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d0e6048a-c528-5a8c-b47d-ee8959e52035" :question/math-academy-id "q-126654" :question/problem "![](images/cb/cbe865077b446bdd97c290cef0924d3f17807a9e1039c9be45f4aa6e4b29cc3c.png)\n\nSuppose that $F$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}$ defined over the region $D$ above. Given that \n\n$$\n{∮}_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=1,\\,{∮}_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=5,\\,{∮}_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=-3,\n$$\n\nwhere ${C}_{1},$ ${C}_{2},$ and ${C}_{3}$ are oriented counterclockwise, calculate the value of ${∮}_{∂D}F\\cdot \\text{d}r.$" :question/worked-solution "To calculate the total circulation of a vector field over the boundary of a region containing holes, we add together the circulations over each *positively oriented* boundary.\n\nRemember that:\n\n- the positive direction is *counterclockwise* for the *outer* curve ${C}_{1}$ (because the region $D$ is always on the left as we traverse ${C}_{1}$ counterclockwise), and\n\n- the positive direction is *clockwise* for the *inner* curves ${C}_{2}$ and ${C}_{3}$ (because the region $D$ is always on the left as we traverse ${C}_{2}$ and ${C}_{3}$ clockwise).\n\nThe diagram below shows the positive orientations of all three curves.\n\n![](images/fd/fd221ae68fc4983fb0a3f7a745154764e5c0081b2b9b66e7f8ad4a150a8b3bc5.png)\n\nNotice that the positive orientations of the inner curves ${C}_{2}$ and ${C}_{3}$ are opposite to those stated in the question.\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{∂D}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={∮}_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r-{∮}_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r-{∮}_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & =1-5-(-3) \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369462 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5d7cf9a0-e8a9-528c-9faf-b2259025de3f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369464} {:answer/id #uuid "ce1af42a-4bf9-5730-a278-44c2bcb271c9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "19" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369465} {:answer/id #uuid "26f52db4-19ad-52bc-bdea-a43ddccabe76" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/val