ively oriented* boundary.\n\nRemember that:\n\n- the positive direction is *counterclockwise* for the *outer* curve ${C}_{1}$ (because the region $D$ is always on the left as we traverse ${C}_{1}$ counterclockwise), and\n\n- the positive direction is *clockwise* for the *inner* curves ${C}_{2}$ and ${C}_{3}$ (because the region $D$ is always on the left as we traverse ${C}_{2}$ and ${C}_{3}$ clockwise).\n\nThe diagram below shows the positive orientations of all three curves.\n\n![](images/85/85a8ea287d2cb8e3b70e332ac444a9e06262fef35369d1bad1f24ea5c9d31687.png)\n\nNotice that the positive orientations of the outer and inner curves are opposite those stated in the question.\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{∂D}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & =-{∮}_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r-{∮}_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r-{∮}_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & =-6-2-(-10) \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369490 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "19b2f076-3c1b-5b41-b54d-acbf22c85f9b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369492} {:answer/id #uuid "dcf840d4-a3b7-5a1f-8ab9-000eaa11d729" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369493} {:answer/id #uuid "544f331d-d37a-57d2-a8ec-3849a346d73c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369494} {:answer/id #uuid "7953c459-d75a-5d7a-a051-42cf152ae33e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369495} {:answer/id #uuid "fc7aedd6-9474-596f-83c6-b28c824b5114" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369496}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7953c459-d75a-5d7a-a051-42cf152ae33e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369495} :answer-field/id #uuid "998a54d8-ef67-567f-aff6-05f6543bcc9b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369491}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d9bfcd0d-e2e4-5ddd-944e-eb308c18a16d" :question/math-academy-id "q-126870" :question/problem "![](images/1f/1f308d1a3ecf3bd0090e22c4f70389311db4db3ff06f7038db0cf7770a8837c7.png)\n\nSuppose that $F$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}$ defined over the region $D$ above. Given that \n\n$$\n{∮}_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=5,\\,{∮}_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=-2,\\,{∮}_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=-9,\n$$\n\nwhere ${C}_{1}$ and ${C}_{2}$ are oriented clockwise and ${C}_{3}$ is oriented counterclockwise, calculate the value of ${∮}_{∂D}F\\cdot \\text{d}r.$" :question/worked-solution "To calculate the total circulation of a vector field over the boundary of a region containing holes, we add together the circulations over each *positively oriented* boundary.\n\nRemember that:\n\n- the positive direction is *counterclockwise* for the *outer* curve ${C}_{1}$ (because the region $D$ is always on the left as we traverse ${C}_{1}$ counterclockwise), and\n\n- the positive direction is *clockwise* for the *inner* curves ${C}_{2}$ and ${C}_{3}$ (because the region $D$ is always on the left as we traverse ${C}_{2}$ and ${C}_{3}$ clockwise).\n\nThe diagram below shows the positive orientations of all three curves.\n\n![](images/2b/2bed1b367cd101e0e90674c619ad87da8c90744df570eeea4d9b0333ccee0de1.png)\n\nNotice that the positive orientations of the outer curve ${C}_{1}$ and the inner curve ${C}_{3}$ are opposite those stated in the question.\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{∂D}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & =-{∮}_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{∮}_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r-{∮}_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & =-5+(-2)-(-9) \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186431348 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "30cf36fe-619f-5674-b7fc-e49a2947c716" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431294} {:answer/id #uuid "4651760e-93e4-58a5-b28b-660b7c2991f7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431304} {:answer/id #uuid "91afb9b0-08b1-5b1f-a644-99141e4a54c6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431317} {:answer/id #uuid "c98ea491-6e86-51b5-b678-6ad024bc1f47" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431331} {:answer/id #uuid "ec3ca01a-910d-58fa-950d-d1c7ecc9fed7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431335}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c98ea491-6e86-51b5-b678-6ad024bc1f47" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431331} :answer-field/id #uuid "d6c20d1b-8be1-5cba-93e4-c9f5ddddc294" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431347}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "084d8c6c-71d5-5e51-afa2-e1398eb868b9" :question/math-academy-id "q-126868" :question/problem "![](images/5e/5e11606627f4b91c977670cf9f919aff8b659cb5b4776cf3d04bb561b50b79fb.png)\n\nSuppose that $F$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}$ defined over the region $D$ above. Given that \n\n$$\n{∮}_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=4,\\,{∮}_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=-6,\\,{∮}_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=-8,\n$$\n\nwhere ${C}_{1},$ ${C}_{2},$ and ${C}_{3}$ are oriented clockwise, calculate the value of ${∮}_{∂D}F\\cdot \\text{d}r.$" :question/worked-solution "To calculate the total circulation of a vector field over the boundary of a region containing holes, we add together the circulations over each *positively oriented* boundary.\n\nRemember that:\n\n- the positive direction is *counterclockwise* for the *outer* curve ${C}_{1}$ (because the region $D$ is always on the left as we traverse ${C}_{1}$ counterclockwise), and\n\n- the positive direction is *clockwise* for the *inner* curves ${C}_{2}$ and ${C}_{3}$ (because the region $D$ is always on the left as we traverse ${C}_{2}$ and ${C}_{3}$ clockwise).\n\nThe diagram below shows the positive orientations of all three curves.\n\n![](images/fa/fa1022d2a1718627898289abdd4d3035a6e06230eea16d0e46832675b2e37def.png)\n\nNotice that the positive orientation of the outer curve ${C}_{1}$ is opposite that of ${C}_{1}$ as stated in the question.\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{∂D}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & =-{∮}_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{∮}_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{∮}_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & =-4+(-6)+(-8) \\\\  & =-18.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186431350 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "64a00c0b-29bc-5b57-873f-d3261312d55f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431311} {:answer/id #uuid "8679841f-eada-5a6a-a61b-82efd37aeeeb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431315} {:answer/id #uuid "88a9b61b-3964-54cb-9363-7eec9cb93295" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "19" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431316} {:answer/id #uuid "caeafa19-d4ce-5934-ae44-0b3839cecee5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431332} {:answer/id #uuid "d4c2bd4b-edba-50d5-a801-73a966ee31b1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431333}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "64a00c0b-29bc-5b57-873f-d3261312d55f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431311} :answer-field/id #uuid "1d54ccd3-fe03-5cf8-b3a3-a6a1f53e813a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431338}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4f71d9a8-f991-5b25-b40f-81e743cd06de" :question/math-academy-id "q-126864" :question/problem "![](images/12/1253965689fbeb233ca3eda553cca8ea8bced9feb81ebc6565c9b65782066b47.png)\n\nSuppose that $F$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}$ defined over the region $D$ above. Given that \n\n$$\n{∮}_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=2,\\,{∮}_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=9,\\,{∮}_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=8,\n$$\n\nwhere ${C}_{1}$ and ${C}_{2}$ are oriented counterclockwise and ${C}_{3}$ is oriented clockwise, calculate the value of ${∮}_{∂D}F\\cdot \\text{d}r.$" :question/worked-solution "To calculate the total circulation of a vector field over the boundary of a region containing holes, we add together the circulations over each *positively oriented* boundary.\n\nRemember that:\n\n- the positive direction is *counterclockwise* for the *outer* curve ${C}_{1}$ (because the region $D$ is always on the left as we traverse ${C}_{1}$ counterclockwise), and\n\n- the positive direction is *clockwise* for the *inner* curves ${C}_{2}$ and ${C}_{3}$ (because the region $D$ is always on the left as we traverse ${C}_{2}$ and ${C}_{3}$ clockwise).\n\nThe diagram below shows the positive orientations of all three curves.\n\n![](images/ca/cad0f92fdd888243562619b410a200ca040ca43f83bfd15fb124c8a8ecae4df6.png)\n\nNotice that the positive orientation of the inner curve ${C}_{2}$ is opposite that of ${C}_{2}$ as stated in the question.\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{∂D}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={∮}_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r-{∮}_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{∮}_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & =2-9+8 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186431351 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ca980d7-830a-5712-906f-d7004a38953f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431285} {:answer/id #uuid "2da41db7-6d0c-5183-9ea5-02fe84e79cce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431292} {:answer/id #uuid "3b720f7a-85ac-5b6e-aa55-b3fe1bb64322" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431298} {:answer/id #uuid "750e0059-fe60-559a-b87a-afcd74ddf718" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431312} {:answer/id #uuid "8173bde3-749c-53ab-95a4-d623b09672f7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431313}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0ca980d7-830a-5712-906f-d7004a38953f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431285} :answer-field/id #uuid "a6feb552-f724-5d46-86df-32005b0ffcc7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431345}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5c72e07b-dd70-5ff7-8f10-0939e87dedc1" :question/math-academy-id "q-126855" :question/problem "![](images/f2/f26ee97bb47430c6a5ed314af007f49415295f682eeb474af33217ef58999758.png)\n\nSuppose that $F$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}$ defined over the region $D$ above. Given that \n\n$$\n{∮}_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=4,\\,{∮}_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=9,\\,{∮}_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=-5,\n$$\n\nwhere ${C}_{1}$ is oriented counterclockwise and ${C}_{2}$ and ${C}_{3}$ are oriented clockwise, calculate the value of ${∮}_{∂D}F\\cdot \\text{d}r.$" :question/worked-solution "To calculate the total circulation of a vector field over the boundary of a region containing holes, we add together the circulations over each *positively oriented* boundary.\n\nRemember that:\n\n- the positive direction is *counterclockwise* for the *outer* curve ${C}_{1}$ (because the region $D$ is always on the left as we traverse ${C}_{1}$ counterclockwise), and\n\n- the positive direction is *clockwise* for the *inner* curves ${C}_{2}$ and ${C}_{3}$ (because the region $D$ is always on the left as we traverse ${C}_{2}$ and ${C}_{3}$ clockwise).\n\nThe diagram below shows the positive orientations of all three curves.\n\n![](images/7b/7b68ab924946d0509b1666b5c0795a7f8987c32aef88ca3ab72746663e9428a8.png)\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{∂D}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={∮}_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{∮}_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{∮}_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & =4+9+(-5) \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186431357 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1d8f90a5-b39b-592d-a1d9-ab32a0cec7c6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431287} {:answer/id #uuid "462f4f66-48fa-5a1b-a234-7ec355e80ccf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431303} {:answer/id #uuid "61820d09-1fb4-5a1a-a196-e88b05cc9b4b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431309} {:answer/id #uuid "62afb4f8-e3ba-552d-b94f-0f8ee6e59d30" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431310} {:answer/id #uuid "b48bbdd2-bf0b-5780-9c2e-2d82fda1acee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431326}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "462f4f66-48fa-5a1b-a234-7ec355e80ccf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431303} :answer-field/id #uuid "23144688-cc3e-5691-a60e-f350dd3406bf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431340}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "fa88e59c-b562-550a-9e35-b904fd919d3f" :question/math-academy-id "q-126861" :question/problem "![](images/ef/efc1df0808af7ab4dacfb3031fdde2132bceaf0e89f88788110d7b5f5ed688bf.png)\n\nSuppose that $F$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}$ defined over the region $D$ above. Given that \n\n$$\n{∮}_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=7,\\,{∮}_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=-3,\\,{∮}_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=2,\n$$\n\nwhere ${C}_{1}$ is oriented counterclockwise and ${C}_{2}$ and ${C}_{3}$ are oriented clockwise, calculate the value of ${∮}_{∂D}F\\cdot \\text{d}r.$" :question/worked-solution "To calculate the total circulation of a vector field over the boundary of a region containing holes, we add together the circulations over each *positively oriented* boundary.\n\nRemember that:\n\n- the positive direction is *counterclockwise* for the *outer* curve ${C}_{1}$ (because the region $D$ is always on the left as we traverse ${C}_{1}$ counterclockwise), and\n\n- the positive direction is *clockwise* for the *inner* curves ${C}_{2}$ and ${C}_{3}$ (because the region $D$ is always on the left as we traverse ${C}_{2}$ and ${C}_{3}$ clockwise).\n\nThe diagram below shows the positive orientations of all three curves.\n\n![](images/f7/f7869b25194e311c08df6c5b647fe9c53f7064c94eaa5d60fa1fa2551a6b58a1.png)\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{∂D}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={∮}_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{∮}_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{∮}_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & =7+(-3)+2 \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Circulation of a Vector Field Over a Region Containing Holes"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133900 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133716 :question/id #uuid "defe326f-f578-568c-a9b3-3d01f67da058" :question/math-academy-id "e-9558" :question/problem "![](images/64/64ba92b021de2ba2f4fc98f1ce76fa0d16b3c8b7ed9ae45237cb8772b111c8b8.png)\n\n  \n\nCalculate \n$\\displaystyle\n\\oint\\limits_{\\partial D} (2x^3y^2-x^5) \\,\\mathrm{d}x + (3x^4y+y^4) \\,\\mathrm{d}y,$\nwhere $D$ is the region shown above." :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_{C} P\\,\\mathrm{d}x + Q\\,\\mathrm{d}y \n= \\iint\\limits_D \\left(\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x} - \\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}\\right)\\,\\textrm d A.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem can be extended to include regions containing holes. In such cases, we can apply Green's theorem the same way as before.\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=2x^3y^2-x^5$ and $Q(x,y)= 3x^4y+y^4.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial y} = 4x^3y, \\qquad \n\\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x} = 12x^3y.\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{∂D}(2{x}^{3}{y}^{2}-{x}^{5})\\,dx+(3{x}^{4}y+{y}^{4})\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}12{x}^{3}y-4{x}^{3}y\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}8{x}^{3}y\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ can be represented in Cartesian coordinates as $\\Delta_1\\setminus\\Delta_2,$ where\n\n- $\\Delta_1 = \\big\\{ (x,y) \\: : \\: -2 \\leq x \\leq 1, -1 \\leq y \\leq 2 \\big\\}$ is the region enclosed by the outer curve, and\n\n- $\\Delta_2 = \\big\\{ (x,y) \\: : \\: -1 \\leq x \\leq 0, 0 \\leq y \\leq 1 \\big\\}$ is the region enclosed by the inner curve.\n\nTherefore, our double integral becomes\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}8{x}^{3}y\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{{Δ}_{1}}8{x}^{3}y\\,\\text{d}A-{∬}_{{Δ}_{2}}8{x}^{3}y\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{2}{\\int }_{-2}^{1}8{x}^{3}y\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y-{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{-1}^{0}8{x}^{3}y\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{1}4{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x{\\int }_{-1}^{2}2y\\,\\text{d}y-{\\int }_{-1}^{0}4{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x{\\int }_{0}^{1}2y\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={x}^{4}\\,{|}_{-2}^{1}\\cdot {y}^{2}\\,{|}_{-1}^{2}-{x}^{4}\\,{|}_{-1}^{0}\\cdot {y}^{2}\\,{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =(1-16)\\cdot (4-1)-(0-1)\\cdot (1-0) \\\\  & =(-15)\\cdot 3-(-1)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-44.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cdd9b289-4285-5958-a9ef-853882a18d2b" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134099 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "18f1a9de-bb0b-570a-b792-70641d11475f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{15\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431286} {:answer/id #uuid "4724eac8-60ba-5daf-b39a-a8285610e36c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431305} {:answer/id #uuid "84acf7b5-dff2-5f83-9da4-34dd8d7b51ee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431314} {:answer/id #uuid "9970035c-1db5-5547-b617-95eb8f16e1ca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{13\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431318} {:answer/id #uuid "9fc187d5-7c8b-56e2-94b6-83f788afb083" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431319}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "18f1a9de-bb0b-570a-b792-70641d11475f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{15\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431286} :answer-field/id #uuid "b24f5ff7-bafc-59bf-b1fe-ef9b2d41540e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130750}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5478739e-f4d5-5eec-8651-b4b73e6b218c" :question/math-academy-id "q-126566" :question/problem "![](images/52/52815b087f869769950d37ab6b95edb75accf030e742ed16b4679b6a41d7d638.png)\n\nCalculate ${∮}_{∂D}-{x}^{2}y\\,dx+x{y}^{2}\\,dy,$ where $D$ is the annular region shown above." :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem can be extended to include regions containing holes. In such cases, we can apply Green's theorem in the same way as before.\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=-{x}^{2}y$ and $Q(x,y)=x{y}^{2}.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-{x}^{2},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}={y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{∂D}-{x}^{2}y\\,dx+x{y}^{2}\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}{y}^{2}-(-{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,1\\le r\\le 2,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nRecall that the change of variables formula for polar coordinates gives\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, our double integral becomes\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{1}^{2}(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}\\cdot r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{1}^{2}{r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\cdot r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{1}^{2}{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}\\theta {\\int }_{1}^{2}{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{2}{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r \\\\  & =2\\pi \\cdot {\\frac{1}{4}{r}^{4}|}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\cdot ({2}^{4}-{1}^{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{15\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134101 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e4e662c6-8699-54e7-97af-a7c14d402002" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "84" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130763} {:answer/id #uuid "f69c5e62-4eaa-562e-b551-1ab0def8e8e1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "138" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130764} {:answer/id #uuid "09f9fc3a-138d-57c4-811a-5930d1b13580" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "120" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130765} {:answer/id #uuid "3d68c829-31e9-571e-a524-13a9c5b60036" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "55" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130766} {:answer/id #uuid "4a1ba910-c08a-502c-9cad-2f9758787120" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "169" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130767}] :answer-field/id #uuid "af970c9a-4925-5b83-89f9-f1d57c4e3ec6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130762} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "aeb67126-f2b3-5385-ae58-f6aa47dd1135" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "55" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369394} {:answer/id #uuid "6a1f51d1-849c-5c40-98ed-fc855b6f7397" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "84" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369395} {:answer/id #uuid "1cbc2426-d8bc-5d9e-b636-6dd81e32cf26" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "138" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369396} {:answer/id #uuid "ee3e82f7-ed7f-5766-8b2b-b24890ff949c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "120" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369397} {:answer/id #uuid "5ba0ac0c-d2ca-5864-b0a8-1497f9993024" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "169" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369398}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1cbc2426-d8bc-5d9e-b636-6dd81e32cf26" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "138" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369396} :answer-field/id #uuid "83851aef-4707-5c37-b41d-06a411ef4f5f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369393}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "23de2113-f2a7-50b0-94a3-c0894b2b5ab4" :question/math-academy-id "q-126645" :question/problem "![](images/6b/6bda5a10df8a1ec081db97e7aa52755796f1d50be5447e3e64f55986116ada6e.png)\n\nCalculate \n${∮}_{∂D}x{y}^{2}\\,dx+({x}^{2}y+x{y}^{2})\\,dy,$\nwhere $D$ is the region shown above." :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem can be extended to include regions containing holes. In such cases, we can apply Green's theorem in the same way as before.\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=x{y}^{2}$ and $Q(x,y)={x}^{2}y+x{y}^{2}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=2xy,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=2xy+{y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{∂D}x{y}^{2}\\,dx+({x}^{2}y+x{y}^{2})\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(2xy+{y}^{2})-2xy\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ can be represented in Cartesian coordinates as ${Δ}_{1}∖{Δ}_{2},$ where\n\n- ${Δ}_{1}={(x,y)\\,:\\,-4\\le x\\le 4,-3\\le y\\le 3}$ is the region enclosed by the outer curve, and\n\n- ${Δ}_{2}={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 2,-1\\le y\\le 2}$ is the region enclosed by the inner curve.\n\nTherefore, our double integral becomes\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{{Δ}_{1}}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A-{∬}_{{Δ}_{2}}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-3}^{3}{\\int }_{-4}^{4}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y-{\\int }_{-1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-4}^{4}\\,\\text{d}x{\\int }_{-3}^{3}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y-{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x{\\int }_{-1}^{2}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =x\\,{|}_{-4}^{4}\\cdot \\frac{{y}^{3}}{3}\\,{|}_{-3}^{3}-x\\,{|}_{0}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{{y}^{3}}{3}{|}_{-1}^{2} \\\\  & =(4-(-4))\\cdot (9-(-9))-(2-0)\\cdot (\\frac{8}{3}-(-\\frac{1}{3})) \\\\  & =8\\cdot 18-2\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =138.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362555 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ff3661cf-178f-52bd-85c7-48d75d20b100" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362557}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ff3661cf-178f-52bd-85c7-48d75d20b100" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362557} :answer-field/id #uuid "53b1b3ff-21b1-5213-8b9c-584dd6a1a1eb" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362556}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "98f26745-fe03-53ee-9421-2717c110e542" :question/math-academy-id "q-169207" :question/problem "![](images/83/8354a964dc235f1c9676754cf7d163cb4a54f05bd5899ff398acb11a4287861d.png)     \n                                \n                            \n\n   \n\n                            Let $∂R$ be the boundary of the region $R,$ shown above. Use Green's theorem to calculate the following integral:\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ${∮}_{∂R}^{}F\\cdot \\text{d}r\\,={∮}_{∂R}^{}3x{y}^{2}\\,dx-{y}^{3}\\,dy\\,=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem can be extended to include regions containing holes. In such cases, we can apply Green's theorem in the same way as before.\n\nAs we saw earlier, we can use Green's theorem to write our line integral in polar coordinates as \n\n$$\n{∮}_{∂R}3x{y}^{2}\\,dx-{y}^{3}\\,dy={∬}_{Δ}-3{r}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nBut this time, our region $R$ (in polar coordinates) is given by \n\n$$\nΔ={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,1\\le r\\le 2,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi }.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{Δ}-3{r}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  & =-3{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{1}^{2}{r}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =-3{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\cdot {\\int }_{1}^{2}{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r \\\\  & =-3{[\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )}{-2}]}_{\\theta =0}^{\\theta =2\\pi }\\cdot {[\\frac{1}{4}{r}^{4}]}_{r=1}^{r=2}\\,\\text{d}r \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}(1-1)\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}({2}^{4}-{1}^{4}) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369504 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f6a80b35-ba1d-5225-b811-45d42e3518c3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{80\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369506} {:answer/id #uuid "e3d29cd2-757a-5f31-a966-786ef1201ef4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{242\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369507} {:answer/id #uuid "9a7bf771-68a8-5635-b443-270be3859a67" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{728\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369508} {:answer/id #uuid "3d1b880d-8ca7-5039-a151-5e9c415014b0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "728\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369509} {:answer/id #uuid "db63a2b7-a407-5f7d-b9b2-4abcfc16c974" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "243\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369510}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9a7bf771-68a8-5635-b443-270be3859a67" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{728\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369508} :answer-field/id #uuid "0fe2c0c3-ccf0-5837-bf7d-eba354f732ab" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369505}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a38e1386-54d8-53c8-80be-ec42c2b70086" :question/math-academy-id "q-126935" :question/problem "![](images/5c/5ca9f071eaeebb0b31fba99a48ca8f08bd6b4b73588838aad7b5f513c283fd6c.png)\n\nCalculate ${∮}_{∂D}-({x}^{4}y+\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{2}{y}^{3})\\,dx+(x{y}^{4}+\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}{y}^{2})\\,dy,$ where $D$ is the annular region shown above." :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem can be extended to include regions containing holes. In such cases, we can apply Green's theorem in the same way as before.\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=-{x}^{4}y-\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{2}{y}^{3}$ and $Q(x,y)=x{y}^{4}+\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}{y}^{2}.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-{x}^{4}-{x}^{2}{y}^{2},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}={y}^{4}+{x}^{2}{y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{∂D}-({x}^{4}y+\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{2}{y}^{3})\\,dx+(x{y}^{4}+\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}{y}^{2})\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}{y}^{4}+{x}^{2}{y}^{2}-(-{x}^{4}-{x}^{2}{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}{x}^{4}+2{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+{y}^{4}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})^{2}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as \n\n$$\nΔ={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,1\\le r\\le 3,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi }.\n$$\n\nRecall that the change of variables formula for polar coordinates gives\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, our double integral becomes\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})^{2}\\,\\text{d}A & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{1}^{3}((r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2})^{2}\\cdot r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{1}^{3}{r}^{4}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )^{2}\\cdot r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{1}^{3}{r}^{5}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}\\theta {\\int }_{1}^{3}{r}^{5}\\,\\text{d}r \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{3}{r}^{5}\\,\\text{d}r \\\\  & =2\\pi \\cdot {\\frac{1}{6}{r}^{6}|}_{1}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\cdot ({3}^{6}-{1}^{6}) \\\\  & =\\frac{728\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369511 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "adb00ccb-3eb2-54ed-b39e-a1b797a275e5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-90\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369513} {:answer/id #uuid "7353f729-db5d-5849-9168-5e4b921191db" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "120\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369514} {:answer/id #uuid "f2684cd6-1f99-5ede-94b6-e3ceba6e28d2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-145\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369515} {:answer/id #uuid "f2d8e429-0275-503d-8cb9-59c096c6a40c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-120\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369516} {:answer/id #uuid "1b918db5-2ba3-5ac0-8eae-fc4b0f13cbc5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "90\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369517}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f2d8e429-0275-503d-8cb9-59c096c6a40c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-120\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369516} :answer-field/id #uuid "1d326558-bc4e-5ef3-84ac-5f28a471d7e6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369512}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "88b73727-8c06-5f8a-b431-35e41d39a38b" :question/math-academy-id "q-126937" :question/problem "![](images/e6/e6252037001e1a5548ede86a16b4ddde8f5aec8fd956bded55a3506f7b533dbe.png)\n\nCalculate ${∮}_{∂D}(\\frac{1}{3}{y}^{3}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\,dx+(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({sin}^{2}⁡y)-\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3})\\,dy,$ where $D$ is the annular region shown above." :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem can be extended to include regions containing holes. In such cases, we can apply Green's theorem in the same way as before.\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=\\frac{1}{3}{y}^{3}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ and $Q(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({sin}^{2}⁡y)-\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}={y}^{2},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=-{x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{∂D}(\\frac{1}{3}{y}^{3}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\,dx+(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({sin}^{2}⁡y)-\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3})\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as \n\n$$\nΔ={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,2\\le r\\le 4,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi }.\n$$\n\nRecall that the change of variables formula for polar coordinates gives\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, our double integral becomes\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{2}^{4}-((r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2})\\cdot r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{2}^{4}-{r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\cdot r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{2}^{4}-{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}\\theta {\\int }_{2}^{4}-{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r \\\\  & =-2\\pi {\\int }_{2}^{4}{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r \\\\  & =-2\\pi \\cdot {\\frac{1}{4}{r}^{4}|}_{2}^{4} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\cdot ({4}^{4}-{2}^{4}) \\\\  & =-120\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369518 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9d05767d-a65e-5d43-a488-57f4105f6090" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369520} {:answer/id #uuid "b9437141-8677-5d36-ade8-56553767eb3c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369521} {:answer/id #uuid "71c53a26-6b18-5e5a-a6b4-14d20c2a1939" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369522} {:answer/id #uuid "c90c6307-93a1-5a42-b489-784db15ed739" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369523} {:answer/id #uuid "d963956c-f4e3-525a-b6b9-91fd86eb5922" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369524}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9d05767d-a65e-5d43-a488-57f4105f6090" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369520} :answer-field/id #uuid "38c1ef24-72d4-585c-9c7b-034e01ca1112" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369519}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "ae4fa615-3a59-509f-96cf-981693bf3db2" :question/math-academy-id "q-126941" :question/problem "![](images/b2/b232a4cddf05a425709c1b3204b69b0126e586b42a6af73b867dd75ee294d961.png)\n\nCalculate \n${∮}_{∂D}({x}^{3}{y}^{3}+x)\\,dx+({x}^{4}{y}^{2}-y)\\,dy,$\nwhere $D$ is the region shown above." :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem can be extended to include regions containing holes. In such cases, we can apply Green's theorem in the same way as before.\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)={x}^{3}{y}^{3}+x$ and $Q(x,y)={x}^{4}{y}^{2}-y.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=3{x}^{3}{y}^{2},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=4{x}^{3}{y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{∂D}({x}^{3}{y}^{3}+x)\\,dx+({x}^{4}{y}^{2}-y)\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}4{x}^{3}{y}^{2}-3{x}^{3}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}{x}^{3}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ can be represented in Cartesian coordinates as ${Δ}_{1}∖{Δ}_{2},$ where\n\n- ${Δ}_{1}={(x,y)\\,:\\,-2\\le x\\le 2,-1\\le y\\le 2}$ is the region enclosed by the outer curve, and\n\n- ${Δ}_{2}={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,0\\le y\\le 1}$ is the region enclosed by the inner curve.\n\nTherefore, our double integral becomes\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}{x}^{3}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{{Δ}_{1}}{x}^{3}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A-{∬}_{{Δ}_{2}}{x}^{3}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{2}{\\int }_{-2}^{2}{x}^{3}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y-{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{3}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{2}{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x{\\int }_{-1}^{2}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y-{\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x{\\int }_{0}^{1}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4}\\,{|}_{-2}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{{y}^{3}}{3}\\,{|}_{-1}^{2}-\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4}\\,{|}_{0}^{1}\\cdot \\frac{{y}^{3}}{3}\\,{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =(4-4)\\cdot (\\frac{8}{3}-(-\\frac{1}{3}))-(\\frac{1}{4}-0)\\cdot (\\frac{1}{3}-0) \\\\  & =0\\cdot 3-\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot \\frac{1}{3} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369525 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "006e746d-7483-59b3-ba9c-8cd280691f9d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369527} {:answer/id #uuid "64fc0003-b805-567e-8d14-5ab416216a60" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369528} {:answer/id #uuid "14561ae1-e3be-5f9d-bccd-fde3e73d6059" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369529} {:answer/id #uuid "da7adf00-3485-5b42-ac23-7efeb71d22ab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{7}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369530} {:answer/id #uuid "89cc8f37-3c67-5183-a3bc-6cf27168df13" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369531}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "89cc8f37-3c67-5183-a3bc-6cf27168df13" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369531} :answer-field/id #uuid "1303c02b-a3ee-52e9-98a2-763dbec18e4b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369526}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "904872cd-e02c-55d9-9792-17de482b0907" :question/math-academy-id "q-126945" :question/problem "![](images/75/754aa1ded10b20362350f3da9e5b4dbe5f9f194c2289af348ff9de34f2fb8cc5.png)\n\nCalculate \n${∮}_{∂D}(x{y}^{2}+{x}^{3})\\,dx+(2{x}^{2}y-{y}^{3})\\,dy,$\nwhere $D$ is the region shown above." :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem can be extended to include regions containing holes. In such cases, we can apply Green's theorem in the same way as before.\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=x{y}^{2}+{x}^{3}$ and $Q(x,y)=2{x}^{2}y-{y}^{3}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=2xy,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=4xy.\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{∂D}(x{y}^{2}+{x}^{3})\\,dx+(2{x}^{2}y-{y}^{3})\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}4xy-2xy\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2xy\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ can be represented in Cartesian coordinates as ${Δ}_{1}∖{Δ}_{2},$ where\n\n- ${Δ}_{1}={(x,y)\\,:\\,-2\\le x\\le 2,-1\\le y\\le 2}$ is the region enclosed by the outer curve, and\n\n- ${Δ}_{2}={(x,y)\\,:\\,-1\\le x\\le 1,0\\le y\\le 1}$ is the region enclosed by the inner curve.\n\nTherefore, our double integral becomes\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}2xy\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{{Δ}_{1}}2xy\\,\\text{d}A-{∬}_{{Δ}_{2}}2xy\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{2}{\\int }_{-2}^{2}2xy\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y-{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}2xy\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{2}2x\\,\\text{d}x{\\int }_{-1}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}y-{\\int }_{-1}^{1}2x\\,\\text{d}x{\\int }_{0}^{1}y\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={x}^{2}\\,{|}_{-2}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}\\,{|}_{-1}^{2}-{x}^{2}\\,{|}_{-1}^{1}\\cdot \\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}\\,{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =(4-4)\\cdot (2-\\frac{1}{2})-(1-1)\\cdot (\\frac{1}{2}-0) \\\\  & =0\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}-0\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186431349 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "247e7c9b-86e2-5f51-8ba9-c9448bc0dc8e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431290} {:answer/id #uuid "408f7865-0008-58e6-85c6-ac75d60946d2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "189" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431301} {:answer/id #uuid "4618a806-c076-56af-a407-64d1881a0e0d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "192" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431302} {:answer/id #uuid "51464593-b024-55b5-8ac6-2bf2b769ffdd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "180" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431306} {:answer/id #uuid "c459028d-7c56-50dd-8fbf-e3321e5298d7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "200" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431330}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "247e7c9b-86e2-5f51-8ba9-c9448bc0dc8e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431290} :answer-field/id #uuid "4a97b187-3262-5c06-b7ed-fed9c7d6dc5c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431342}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "420adaa9-fbd3-5686-bca6-a55b93dae680" :question/math-academy-id "q-126939" :question/problem "![](images/b1/b1a063fd261e790a8c3a56f741d0356f963300c3fbd88ddde03aa1744ef81053.png)\n\nCalculate ${∮}_{∂D}({x}^{2}-{y}^{4})\\,dx+({x}^{3}y-x{y}^{3})\\,dy,$ where $D$ is the annular region shown above." :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem can be extended to include regions containing holes. In such cases, we can apply Green's theorem in the same way as before.\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)={x}^{2}-{y}^{4}$ and $Q(x,y)=({x}^{3}y-x{y}^{3}).$ So,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-4{y}^{3},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=3{x}^{2}y-{y}^{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{∂D}({x}^{2}-{y}^{4})\\,dx+({x}^{3}y-x{y}^{3})\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}3{x}^{2}y-{y}^{3}-(-4{y}^{3})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}3{x}^{2}y+3{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}3y({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,1\\le r\\le 4,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nRecall that the change of variables formula for polar coordinates gives\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, our double integral becomes\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}3y({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{1}^{4}3\\cdot r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\cdot ((r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2})\\cdot r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{1}^{4}3{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\cdot {r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{1}^{4}3{r}^{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta {\\int }_{1}^{4}3{r}^{4}\\,\\text{d}r \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,{|}_{0}^{2\\pi }\\cdot \\frac{3{r}^{5}}{5}\\,{|}_{1}^{4} \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\pi )-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0))\\cdot (\\frac{3(4)^{5}}{5}-\\frac{3(1)^{5}}{5}) \\\\  & =(1-1)\\cdot (\\frac{3(4)^{5}}{5}-\\frac{3(1)^{5}}{5}) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186431353 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "236daf0a-8fec-5986-adef-4fec2089883e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "100\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431289} {:answer/id #uuid "2980d347-4cd0-508a-a19e-609126e74ead" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "80\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431291} {:answer/id #uuid "b95f6d7e-5d5f-5174-809f-affea38f8fa2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "140\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431328} {:answer/id #uuid "c0d3bde0-ed52-52bf-9585-a17e40e4ed16" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "120\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431329} {:answer/id #uuid "f2034a12-3dc0-57c4-b9a4-d7673fd61b74" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "160\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431336}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f2034a12-3dc0-57c4-b9a4-d7673fd61b74" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "160\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431336} :answer-field/id #uuid "2b281059-95fd-5ff3-8dbf-e28f1dfbb9f1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431341}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "93158d64-29fd-580d-9bf5-f56c5e2adb43" :question/math-academy-id "q-126938" :question/problem "![](images/5c/5ca9f071eaeebb0b31fba99a48ca8f08bd6b4b73588838aad7b5f513c283fd6c.png)\n\nCalculate ${∮}_{∂D}-({x}^{2}y+{y}^{3})\\,dx+({x}^{3}+x{y}^{2})\\,dy,$ where $D$ is the annular region shown above." :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem can be extended to include regions containing holes. In such cases, we can apply Green's theorem in the same way as before.\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=-{x}^{2}y-{y}^{3}$ and $Q(x,y)={x}^{3}+x{y}^{2}.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-{x}^{2}-3{y}^{2},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=3{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{∂D}-({x}^{2}y+{y}^{3})\\,dx+({x}^{3}+x{y}^{2})\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}3{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-(-{x}^{2}-3{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}4{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}4({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,1\\le r\\le 3,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nRecall that the change of variables formula for polar coordinates gives\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, our double integral becomes\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}4({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A & =4{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{1}^{3}(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}\\cdot r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{1}^{3}{r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\cdot r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{1}^{3}{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}\\theta {\\int }_{1}^{3}{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r \\\\  & =8\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{2}{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r \\\\  & =8\\pi \\cdot {\\frac{1}{4}{r}^{4}|}_{1}^{3} \\\\  & =2\\pi \\cdot ({3}^{4}-{1}^{4}) \\\\  & =160\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186431355 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2f39220d-1633-509c-81a8-5131d70771c2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "42" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431293} {:answer/id #uuid "39f62df8-11d8-553d-8fdd-204067835d8d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "36" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431296} {:answer/id #uuid "3c2c4c9a-4ad0-5f0f-96f5-e727f1d26528" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431299} {:answer/id #uuid "59acde88-fd8f-54be-a537-e285b05463de" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431307} {:answer/id #uuid "5bef94c4-e6c8-5f82-b55a-2a267716a18a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "27" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431308}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3c2c4c9a-4ad0-5f0f-96f5-e727f1d26528" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431299} :answer-field/id #uuid "d21d5399-1389-5996-9269-7e769b0bb606" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431346}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "d01c58ab-6639-5fe1-a3d3-01aeb78816a2" :question/math-academy-id "q-126942" :question/problem "![](images/db/dbe6009d42623d3b3cd9f05380159bb0f4b8f22b920c1ef5d4d82b5dbf152d4a.png)\n\nCalculate \n${∮}_{∂D}{y}^{2}(x+1)\\,dx+(3{x}^{2}y+2xy)\\,dy,$\nwhere $D$ is the region shown above." :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem can be extended to include regions containing holes. In such cases, we can apply Green's theorem in the same way as before.\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)={y}^{2}(x+1)$ and $Q(x,y)=3{x}^{2}y+2xy.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=2y(x+1),\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=6xy+2y.\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{∂D}{y}^{2}(x+1)\\,dx+(3{x}^{2}y+2xy)\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(6xy+2y)-2y(x+1)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}4xy\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ can be represented in Cartesian coordinates as ${Δ}_{1}∖{Δ}_{2},$ where\n\n- ${Δ}_{1}=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 3,-1\\le y\\le 2\\}$ is the region enclosed by the outer curve, and\n\n- ${Δ}_{2}=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,1\\le x\\le 2,0\\le y\\le 1\\}$ is the region enclosed by the inner curve.\n\nTherefore, our double integral becomes\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}4xy\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{{Δ}_{1}}4xy\\,\\text{d}A-{∬}_{{Δ}_{2}}4xy\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{3}4xy\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y-{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{1}^{2}4xy\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}2x\\,\\text{d}x{\\int }_{-1}^{2}2y\\,\\text{d}y-{\\int }_{1}^{2}2x\\,\\text{d}x{\\int }_{0}^{1}2y\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={x}^{2}\\,{|}_{0}^{3}\\cdot {y}^{2}\\,{|}_{-1}^{2}-{x}^{2}\\,{|}_{1}^{2}\\cdot {y}^{2}\\,{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =(9-0)\\cdot (4-1)-(4-1)\\cdot (1-0) \\\\  & =9\\cdot 3-3\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =24.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186431356 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3d37215b-1943-5905-97aa-565540294dc2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431300} {:answer/id #uuid "a2b7de02-99e4-5e8d-9b2d-adc7d46bc083" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431320} {:answer/id #uuid "a55fde2d-708a-592b-8240-a4b285208e2b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431321} {:answer/id #uuid "adc8174a-05b0-5d22-bae6-7c69965d1aa2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431322} {:answer/id #uuid "b3b1df00-1a0c-583b-9b5b-65ba53909dfe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431325}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b3b1df00-1a0c-583b-9b5b-65ba53909dfe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431325} :answer-field/id #uuid "5e0020cb-0a2c-5126-8edf-5125f13da402" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431343}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "f20c5355-cea7-5f07-9f8f-ed2bfe376f88" :question/math-academy-id "q-126946" :question/problem "![](images/9b/9be396fd57559bb77f92d5a0421f3a7cdca577203904e577da0caf5326caac91.png)\n\nCalculate \n${∮}_{∂D}(2{x}^{2}{y}^{4}-x)\\,dx+(4{x}^{3}{y}^{3}-{y}^{2})\\,dy,$\nwhere $D$ is the region shown above." :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem can be extended to include regions containing holes. In such cases, we can apply Green's theorem in the same way as before.\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=2{x}^{2}{y}^{4}-x$ and $Q(x,y)=4{x}^{3}{y}^{3}-{y}^{2}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=8{x}^{2}{y}^{3},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=12{x}^{2}{y}^{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{∂D}(2{x}^{2}{y}^{4}-x)\\,dx+(4{x}^{3}{y}^{3}-{y}^{2})\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}12{x}^{2}{y}^{3}-8{x}^{2}{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}4{x}^{2}{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ can be represented in Cartesian coordinates as ${Δ}_{1}∖{Δ}_{2},$ where\n\n- ${Δ}_{1}=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,-1\\le x\\le 2,-2\\le y\\le 2\\}$ is the region enclosed by the outer curve, and\n\n- ${Δ}_{2}=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,0\\le y\\le 1\\}$ is the region enclosed by the inner curve.\n\nTherefore, our double integral becomes\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}4{x}^{2}{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{{Δ}_{1}}4{x}^{2}{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}A-{∬}_{{Δ}_{2}}4{x}^{2}{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{2}{\\int }_{-1}^{2}4{x}^{2}{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y-{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}4{x}^{2}{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{2}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x{\\int }_{-2}^{2}4{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}y-{\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x{\\int }_{0}^{1}4{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}\\,{|}_{-1}^{2}\\cdot {y}^{4}\\,{|}_{-2}^{2}-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}\\,{|}_{0}^{1}\\cdot {y}^{4}\\,{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =(\\frac{8}{3}-(-\\frac{1}{3}))\\cdot (16-16)-(\\frac{1}{3}-0)\\cdot (1-0) \\\\  & =3\\cdot 0-\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Applying Green's Theorem to Regions With Holes: Advanced Cases"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133926 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133486 :question/id #uuid "4f12e12d-37b3-5c41-9284-bc369ccd4c0f" :question/math-academy-id "e-12421" :question/problem "Calculate the circulation $\\Gamma$ of the vector field $\\mathbf F(x,y,z) = y \\,\\mathbf{i} -x \\,\\mathbf{j} + z\\,\\mathbf{k}$ along the closed curve $C$ given by $\\mathbf r(t) =  \\sin (\\pi t)\\,\\mathbf{i} + \\cos (\\pi t)\\, \\mathbf{j} + t(2-t)\\,\\mathbf{k}$ for $t \\in [0,2].$" :question/worked-solution "The circulation $\\Gamma$ measures the total work done by a force $\\mathbf F$ in moving a particle along a *closed* curve $C.$\nIt is given by the formula\n\n$$\n\\Gamma = \\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf F \\cdot \\textrm{d} \\mathbf r\n= \\int_a^b \\mathbf F(r(t)) \\cdot \\mathbf r'(t) \\; \\textrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx = \\sin (\\pi t), \\qquad\ny = \\cos (\\pi t), \\qquad\nz = t(2-t) = 2t-t^2.\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =y\\,i-x\\,j+z\\,k \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)\\,i-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)\\,j+(2t-{t}^{2})\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $\\mathbf r'(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t))\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t))\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t-{t}^{2})\\,k \\\\  & =\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)\\,i-\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)\\,j+2(1-t)\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =(\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)\\,i-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)\\,j+(2t-{t}^{2})\\,k\\,)\\cdot (\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)\\,i-\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)\\,j+2(1-t)\\,k) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)\\cdot \\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)+(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t))\\cdot (-\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t))+(2t-{t}^{2})\\cdot 2(1-t) \\\\  & =\\pi {cos}^{2}⁡(\\pi t)+\\pi {sin}^{2}⁡(\\pi t)+2(2t-{t}^{2})(1-t) \\\\  & =\\pi ({cos}^{2}⁡(\\pi t)+{sin}^{2}⁡(\\pi t))+2(2t-3{t}^{2}+{t}^{3}) \\\\  & =\\pi +4t-6{t}^{2}+2{t}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Γ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\pi +4t-6{t}^{2}+2{t}^{3}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={[\\pi t+2{t}^{2}-2{t}^{3}+\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{4}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =[2\\pi +8-16+8]-[0] \\\\  & =2\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "74196b81-0e52-5d10-a099-ccd849d03a98" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134129 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3f135681-35a3-57d6-b400-22964cf554ee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279148} {:answer/id #uuid "3f1a53d4-a81b-5701-8651-a106eb9967eb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279149} {:answer/id #uuid "494799ab-eb22-5d77-8cb0-046c691cba3b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279245} {:answer/id #uuid "5aa0d2e3-2989-5892-b7f3-ebba5dfcf3d6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279428} {:answer/id #uuid "d0ee7f1e-c3cb-5620-b310-57d2b69f3133" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280431}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3f135681-35a3-57d6-b400-22964cf554ee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279148} :answer-field/id #uuid "f075172d-b32a-5456-a8d7-c4de03227a4c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281258}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "73fef5f2-e793-56dc-a75a-943258eb19b8" :question/math-academy-id "q-161925" :question/problem "Calculate the circulation $Γ$ of the vector field $F(x,y,z)=y\\,i-x\\,j$ along the closed curve $C$ given by $r(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)\\,j+(1-{t}^{2})\\,k$ for $t\\in [-1,1).$" :question/worked-solution "The circulation $Γ$ measures the total work done by a force $F$ in moving a particle along a *closed* curve $C.$\nIt is given by the formula\n\n$$\nΓ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t),\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t),\\,z=1-{t}^{2}.\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =y\\,i-x\\,j \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)\\,i-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t))\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t))\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1-{t}^{2})\\,k \\\\  & =-\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)\\,i+\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)\\,j-2t\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)\\,i-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)\\,j)\\cdot (-\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)\\,i+\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)\\,j-2t\\,k) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)\\cdot (-\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t))+(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t))\\cdot \\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)+0\\cdot (-2t) \\\\  & =-\\pi {sin}^{2}⁡(\\pi t)-\\pi {cos}^{2}⁡(\\pi t) \\\\  & =-\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Γ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}(-\\pi )\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-\\pi [t]_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =-\\pi (1-(-1)) \\\\  & =-2\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134133 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "91e141a3-db78-5e44-a461-551fe7a0bece" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{60}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130955} {:answer/id #uuid "ad54536a-b558-5c60-b187-fd8fa41d6e02" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130956} {:answer/id #uuid "e0ca6443-99c2-586e-b058-eaae89edfbc4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130957} {:answer/id #uuid "2e445657-7a49-56a3-a596-aad6a22d87db" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{100}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130958} {:answer/id #uuid "5e35f75a-b843-5afb-af33-d8260e24ea04" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "100" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130959}] :answer-field/id #uuid "3d428aec-71bd-5e76-8cd2-9b96389f1f6c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130954}] :question/id #uuid "4bba2c4b-bfab-581e-aa67-d4d398fc018f" :question/math-academy-id "q-162238" :question/problem "Calculate the circulation $Γ$ of the vector field $F(x, y, z) = (y - x)i$ along the closed curve $C$ given by $r(t) = (t^{2} - t)i + (t^{3} - t)j + \\sin (2πt)k$ for $t ∈ [0, 1)$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281416 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c03a648-3276-51bd-9904-df1d4522549b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278709} {:answer/id #uuid "0d190dfe-46ce-5dc3-b82b-670060383d50" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278713} {:answer/id #uuid "2f78edf6-ba63-5eb2-ac9e-cec1baa76b5a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279006} {:answer/id #uuid "7e9d76f8-5364-55b7-bd19-13182f53be56" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279732} {:answer/id #uuid "dacdb6c9-01bb-5071-9c74-09822a99f555" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280523}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0d190dfe-46ce-5dc3-b82b-670060383d50" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278713} :answer-field/id #uuid "565bb2c5-d8e2-5a10-ab5d-91f31338f51b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280978}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c14a191e-1e95-5ff6-80a2-30d79cf0bf66" :question/math-academy-id "q-162220" :question/problem "Calculate the circulation $Γ$ of the vector field $F(x,y,z)=y\\,i+x\\,j$ along the closed curve $C$ given by $r(t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,k$ for $t\\in [0,2\\pi ).$" :question/worked-solution "The circulation $Γ$ measures the total work done by a force $F$ in moving a particle along a *closed* curve $C.$\nIt is given by the formula\n\n$$\nΓ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,z=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =y\\,i+x\\,j \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,k \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j)\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,k) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)+0\\cdot 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\  & ={cos}^{2}⁡t-{sin}^{2}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Γ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }({cos}^{2}⁡t-{sin}^{2}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =[\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)}{2}]_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281421 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2699a966-6b46-57cf-a47c-22a3c667651c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278937} {:answer/id #uuid "32391712-510d-5554-9a81-b26d6930e9b1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279032} {:answer/id #uuid "7bcd3745-9952-55fa-8d98-6a218c3df910" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279695} {:answer/id #uuid "d4655d09-3913-5973-b875-0e396a2fcd45" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280464} {:answer/id #uuid "dcaa048d-a79a-598e-9189-af11a04e4488" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280540}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dcaa048d-a79a-598e-9189-af11a04e4488" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280540} :answer-field/id #uuid "d03b1767-6387-503d-af9b-02fb2cde0ee3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281199}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "fd877a62-b477-55ef-9e5a-194da8c5c887" :question/math-academy-id "q-162230" :question/problem "Calculate the circulation $Γ$ of the vector field $F(x,y,z)=y\\,i-x\\,j+z\\,k$ along the closed curve $C$ given by $r(t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)\\,j+{t}^{2}\\,k$ for $t\\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ).$" :question/worked-solution "The circulation $Γ$ measures the total work done by a force $F$ in moving a particle along a *closed* curve $C.$\nIt is given by the formula\n\n$$\nΓ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t),\\,y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t),\\,z={t}^{2}.\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =y\\,i-x\\,j+z\\,k \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)\\,i-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)\\,j+{t}^{2}\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t))\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t))\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2})\\,k \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)\\,i-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)\\,j+2t\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)\\,i-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)\\,j+{t}^{2}\\,k)\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)\\,i-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)\\,j+2t\\,k) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)+(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t))\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t))+{t}^{2}\\cdot 2t \\\\  & ={cos}^{2}⁡(\\pi t)+{sin}^{2}⁡(t)+2{t}^{3} \\\\  & =1+2{t}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Γ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }(1+2{t}^{3})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={[t+\\frac{{t}^{4}}{2}]}_{-\\pi }^{\\pi } \\\\  & =((\\pi -(-\\pi ))+\\frac{{\\pi }^{4}-(-\\pi )^{4}}{2}) \\\\  & =2\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281422 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "40f453c1-1269-5d7b-a9f7-d8c20a89e598" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279168} {:answer/id #uuid "7c949669-3c80-5476-91b1-fc3b5d631b2c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279704} {:answer/id #uuid "a6e227ef-2347-5b50-9e1d-289d72ee8083" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280068} {:answer/id #uuid "d01a006d-cf1e-5f29-84c5-eff90c285d83" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280422} {:answer/id #uuid "d6aa8cae-8f41-5bb7-8dbc-0f01c9e8efb4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280487}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d6aa8cae-8f41-5bb7-8dbc-0f01c9e8efb4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280487} :answer-field/id #uuid "e8079c5b-3e54-561a-bcb4-10bee6629161" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281245}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "13da6f40-f5bf-549b-a97f-20a44b83344f" :question/math-academy-id "q-162235" :question/problem "Calculate the circulation $Γ$ of the vector field $F(x,y,z)=(x+y)\\,i-z\\,k$ along the closed curve $C$ given by $r(t)=(1-{t}^{2})\\,i+{t}^{2}\\,j+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)\\,k$ for $t\\in [-1,1).$" :question/worked-solution "The circulation $Γ$ measures the total work done by a force $F$ in moving a particle along a *closed* curve $C.$\nIt is given by the formula\n\n$$\nΓ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=1-{t}^{2},\\,y={t}^{2},\\,z=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t).\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =(x+y)\\,i-z\\,k \\\\  & =(1-{t}^{2}+{t}^{2})\\,i-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)\\,k \\\\  & =i-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1-{t}^{2})\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2})\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t))\\,k \\\\  & =-2t\\,i+2t\\,j+\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =(i-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)\\,k)\\cdot (-2t\\,i+2t\\,j+\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)\\,k) \\\\  & =1\\cdot (-2t)+0\\cdot 2t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)\\cdot \\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t) \\\\  & =-2t+\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Γ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}(-2t+\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi t))\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={[-{t}^{2}-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\pi t)}{4}]}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =(-1-\\frac{1}{4})-(-1-\\frac{1}{4}) \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281424 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1107e5cc-b9e4-51b4-b110-09cc2b0cd618" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278747} {:answer/id #uuid "3ffec3d9-ca96-573b-8802-6b5844531fec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279155} {:answer/id #uuid "92a000d5-a346-5920-a484-8ed38558a27d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279894} {:answer/id #uuid "f0d0e41b-2046-5460-a0ac-9936085c2a9d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280693} {:answer/id #uuid "f3b31ed2-8b79-55de-ae35-ed2256356bcc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280725}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3ffec3d9-ca96-573b-8802-6b5844531fec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279155} :answer-field/id #uuid "5895b727-7ab1-5288-96b4-772760ec6b05" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280984}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b7cf2656-c8b3-55d8-81c3-17aef82ea92a" :question/math-academy-id "q-162237" :question/problem "Calculate the circulation $Γ$ of the vector field $F(x,y,z)=-z\\,j+y\\,k$ along the closed curve $C$ given by $r(t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,k$ for $t\\in [0,2\\pi ).$" :question/worked-solution "The circulation $Γ$ measures the total work done by a force $F$ in moving a particle along a *closed* curve $C.$\nIt is given by the formula\n\n$$\nΓ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,z=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =-z\\,j+y\\,k \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,k \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,k)\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,k) \\\\  & =0\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡t+(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\  & ={sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t. \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Γ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }1\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =[t]_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =2\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186431183 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "11019ac0-2397-5082-8da2-e2cda61c67f5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431149} {:answer/id #uuid "2ca8c25f-0afe-549b-bd9e-0579f77dfd35" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431152} {:answer/id #uuid "617b3f2e-e124-52d6-b054-729c57763e06" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431157} {:answer/id #uuid "8493f5bf-0293-5a89-b43a-eae5ba48387d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431159} {:answer/id #uuid "e69380fc-03e3-562e-97ec-2bf770b22d2d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431174}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2ca8c25f-0afe-549b-bd9e-0579f77dfd35" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431152} :answer-field/id #uuid "e84d6437-9ec9-5b4b-a3b3-8f14a689f608" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431181}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1d416162-8b5b-53c3-aa15-9f678fcebfbf" :question/math-academy-id "q-162229" :question/problem "Calculate the circulation $Γ$ of the vector field $F(x,y,z)=-y\\,i+x\\,j$ along the closed curve $C$ given by $r(t)=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)\\,j+2\\,k$ for $t\\in [0,2).$" :question/worked-solution "The circulation $Γ$ measures the total work done by a force $F$ in moving a particle along a *closed* curve $C.$\nIt is given by the formula\n\n$$\nΓ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t),\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t),\\,z=2.\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =-y\\,i+x\\,j \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)\\,i+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t))\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t))\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2)\\,k \\\\  & =-2\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)\\,i+\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)\\,j+0\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)\\,i+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)\\,j)\\cdot (-2\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)\\,i+\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)\\,j+0\\,k) \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)\\cdot (-2\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t))+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)\\cdot \\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)+0\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =2\\pi {sin}^{2}⁡(\\pi t)+2\\pi {cos}^{2}⁡(\\pi t) \\\\  & =2\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Γ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}2\\pi \\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi [t]_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =2\\pi (2-0) \\\\  & =4\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186431188 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "58c1fd16-ca29-5709-a267-219424eaaabc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431156} {:answer/id #uuid "8c03109d-b971-53ee-a86a-7e35ed350bb0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431160} {:answer/id #uuid "adea9c1e-27a3-5208-9dd6-795f2fa57e6d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431165} {:answer/id #uuid "b705117a-dca8-5892-b76c-7de8a8a5594d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431169} {:answer/id #uuid "d1fa4475-9d5e-511a-8566-852d47df5af6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431171}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b705117a-dca8-5892-b76c-7de8a8a5594d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431169} :answer-field/id #uuid "42e70451-3a35-5ee4-bde4-603fef9a0a12" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431178}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9f4551bd-d0a1-5978-99b9-17e8a12314a5" :question/math-academy-id "q-162228" :question/problem "Calculate the circulation $Γ$ of the vector field $F(x,y,z)=z\\,j-y\\,k$ along the closed curve $C$ given by $r(t)=({t}^{2}-t)\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\pi t)\\,j+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi t)\\,k$ for $t\\in [0,1)$" :question/worked-solution "The circulation $Γ$ measures the total work done by a force $F$ in moving a particle along a *closed* curve $C.$\nIt is given by the formula\n\n$$\nΓ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx={t}^{2}-t,\\,y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\pi t),\\,z=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi t).\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =z\\,j-y\\,k \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi t)\\,j-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\pi t)\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}-t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\pi t))\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi t))\\,k \\\\  & =(2t-1)\\,i-2\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi t)\\,j+2\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\pi t)\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi t)\\,j-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\pi t)\\,k)\\cdot ((2t-1)\\,i-2\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi t)\\,j+2\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\pi t)\\,k) \\\\  & =0\\cdot (2t-1)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi t)\\cdot (-2\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi t))+(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\pi t))\\cdot 2\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\pi t) \\\\  & =-2\\pi {sin}^{2}⁡(2\\pi t)-2\\pi {cos}^{2}⁡(2\\pi t) \\\\  & =-2\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Γ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(-2\\pi )\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-2\\pi [t]_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =-2\\pi (1-0) \\\\  & =-2\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Circulation Over Space Curves"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133927 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133487 :question/id #uuid "87c9cf21-797c-5893-a81b-580685331739" :question/math-academy-id "e-12422" :question/problem "Use Stokes' theorem to evaluate \n\n$$\n\\displaystyle{\\oint\\limits_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot\\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{r}},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\textrm{curl}\\,\\mathbf{F} = \\langle -xz, \\: -yz, \\: x+z^2 \\rangle,$ and $C$ is the intersection of the paraboloid $z=x^2+y^2$ and the plane $z=1.$ The curve $C$ is oriented counter-clockwise when viewed from above." :question/worked-solution "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by a simple, closed, piecewise-smooth boundary $C$ with positive orientation. If a vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $S,$ then Stokes' theorem states that\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_{C} \\mathbf{F}\\cdot\\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{r}\n= \\iint\\limits_S \\textrm{curl}\\,\\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{S}.\n$$\n\nThere are many surfaces whose boundary is the curve $C$ representing the intersection of the paraboloid $z=x^2+y^2$ and the plane $z=1.$ One such surface would be the plane $S$ with the equation \n\n$$\nz = g(x,y) = 1.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the partial derivatives of the graph $z=g(x,y){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(1)=0 \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(1)=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the equation $z=g(x,y)$ into the expression for $\\textrm{curl}\\,\\mathbf{F}{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{curl}\\,F\\,{|}_{z=g(x,y)} & =⟨-xz,\\,-yz,\\,{z}^{2}+x⟩{|}_{z=1} \\\\  & =⟨{{-x}_{⏟}}_{P},\\,{{-y}_{⏟}}_{Q},\\,{{1+x}_{⏟}}_{R}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-(-x)\\cdot 0-(-y)\\cdot 0+1+x)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(1+x)\\,\\text{d}A,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is region enclosed by the projection of $C$ onto the $xy$-plane.\n\nNow, note that the projection of $C$ onto the $xy$-plane is the circle $x^2+y^2=1$ of radius $1$ centered at the origin. Therefore, using the change of variables to polar coordinates\n\n$$\nx = r \\cos\\theta, \\qquad\ny = r \\sin\\theta, \\qquad\n\\textrm{d}A = r \\, \\textrm{d}r \\textrm{d}\\theta,\n$$\n\n \n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}(1+x)\\,\\text{d}A & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(1+r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )\\cdot r\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,\\text{d}r \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }r+{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,\\text{d}r \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{[r\\theta +{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ]}_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}r \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}2\\pi r\\,\\text{d}r \\\\  & =\\pi [{r}^{2}]_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8f0629bb-3a37-5ab8-8449-50789b295c7a" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134130 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "87bdc104-c078-582d-af00-3129a1dba874" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "π" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130937} {:answer/id #uuid "8c9be2ce-59b0-5818-bb2c-9478c81a6954" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- π" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130938} {:answer/id #uuid "c97d45ae-ae12-5c14-924c-d56fbb9046f0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130939} {:answer/id #uuid "10779001-334e-5e85-9429-5f9386f45a97" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2π" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130940} {:answer/id #uuid "2b4091c5-96c5-5679-9d29-5647396cd597" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{π}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130941}] :answer-field/id #uuid "0b2becff-6366-549e-8db3-182572eab4ad" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130936}] :question/id #uuid "ee52e831-6d2f-5771-b93b-7f7fa07dc36b" :question/math-academy-id "q-161926" :question/problem "Use Stokes' theorem to evaluate\n$∮_(C)F \\cdot dr$,\nwhere $curlF = ⟨0, 0,-2y⟩$, and $C$ is the intersection of the cylinder $x^{2} + y^{2} = 1$ and the plane $z = 4 - x - 2y$. The curve $C$ is oriented counter-clockwise when viewed from above."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134131 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "89def526-2f68-5214-92a5-30cfa9986621" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{1}\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{1}4 x 3 + 4 x y 2 - 40 xdydx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130943} {:answer/id #uuid "b93aa029-cebb-504c-9cf0-03532112bd4e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{1}\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{1 - x}4 x 3 + 4 x y 2 - 40 xdydx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130944} {:answer/id #uuid "eeea64cd-f2a9-50a4-92af-3714133868f5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{1}\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{x}20 - 4 x 2 + 4 y 2dydx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130945} {:answer/id #uuid "2b0fd797-ddad-5d2d-8cb6-6b915ac256d6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{1}\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{1}20 - 4 x 2 + 4 y 2dydx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130946} {:answer/id #uuid "72429bbc-4c1f-5953-af68-a72527d62038" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{1}\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{1 - x}20 - 4 x 2 + 4 y 2dydx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130947}] :answer-field/id #uuid "1b4f178d-ab86-5fbd-b750-5b059091e2bb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130942} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a96e8d31-77ad-5052-9b6a-9faf559523d5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{x}\\,20-4{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389231} {:answer/id #uuid "eb0e733a-0b54-50ae-a405-756c459c5edb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1-x}\\,4{x}^{3}+4x{y}^{2}-40x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389232} {:answer/id #uuid "615d7f91-8451-58fb-9b50-6912a6fa4329" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1-x}\\,20-4{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389233} {:answer/id #uuid "2375212a-a45b-5bd5-94fc-2601931c58e2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\,4{x}^{3}+4x{y}^{2}-40x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389234} {:answer/id #uuid "41c2840c-096c-53fe-8ab2-a18813e80463" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\,20-4{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389235}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "eb0e733a-0b54-50ae-a405-756c459c5edb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1-x}\\,4{x}^{3}+4x{y}^{2}-40x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389232} :answer-field/id #uuid "8986131b-decf-5b3d-8f27-aafe80a954f8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389230}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d82e0bcb-c8b4-5aa2-9f3c-8a84246a499f" :question/math-academy-id "q-161927" :question/problem "Let $C$ be the intersection of the paraboloid $z=10-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}$ and the boundary of the infinite vertical column enclosed by the planes $x+y=1,$ $x=0,$ and $y=0.$ Given that the curve $C$ is oriented counter-clockwise when viewed from above and $\\text{curl}\\,F=⟨-2z,\\,0,\\,0⟩,$ the circulation of $F$ along $C$ can be written as \n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by a simple, closed, piecewise-smooth boundary $C$ with positive orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $S,$ then Stokes' theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nThere are many surfaces whose boundary is the curve $C$ representing the intersection of the paraboloid $z=10-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}$ and the boundary of our infinite vertical column. One such surface is our paraboloid $S$ with the equation \n\n$$\nz=g(x,y)=10-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the partial derivatives of the graph $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(10-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2})=-2x \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(10-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2})=-2y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the equation $z=g(x,y)$ into expression for $\\text{curl}\\,F:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{curl}\\,F\\,{|}_{z=g(x,y)} & =⟨-2z,\\,0,\\,0⟩{|}_{z=10-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =⟨-2(10-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}),\\,0,\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{{2{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}-20}_{⏟}}_{P},\\,{{0}_{⏟}}_{Q},\\,{{0}_{⏟}}_{R}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-(2{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}-20)\\cdot (-2x)-0\\cdot (-2y)+0)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(4{x}^{3}+4x{y}^{2}-40x)\\,\\text{d}A,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is region enclosed by the projection of $C$ onto the $xy$-plane, shown below.\n\n![](images/c1/c181e209cca01a1d6e372caa7f7810a5fa4aa9f177595bd92f937a7cba8b8a75.png)\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}(4{x}^{3}+4x{y}^{2}-40x)\\,\\text{d}A & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1-x}\\,4{x}^{3}+4x{y}^{2}-40x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235540 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a292d0ec-0d72-578f-ac47-e9d6b19ac77c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2xy-2x-{y}^{2}+8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234790}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a292d0ec-0d72-578f-ac47-e9d6b19ac77c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2xy-2x-{y}^{2}+8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234790} :answer-field/id #uuid "627ed6f9-5e55-5f96-a07b-04dde4b0b9fe" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235076}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6b5155bc-b492-5617-9be0-0783e4c496d1" :question/math-academy-id "q-168625" :question/problem "Let $C$ be the boundary of the top (tilted) surface of our solid. The curve $C$ is oriented counter-clockwise when viewed from above, as shown in the diagram. Use Stokes' theorem to write down the integral that gives the circulation of the air along $C.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        The required circulation is ${∮}_{C}^{}F\\cdot dr\\,={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                         \n$\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x$" :question/worked-solution "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by a simple, closed, piecewise-smooth boundary $C$ with positive orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $S,$ then Stokes' theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nThere are many surfaces whose boundary is the curve $C.$ One such surface is our top (tilted) surface ${S}_{T}$ with the equation \n\n$$\nz=g(x,y)=4-x-y.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the partial derivatives of the graph $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4-x-y)=-1 \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(4-x-y)=-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the equation $z=g(x,y)$ into expression for $\\text{curl}\\,F:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{curl}\\,F\\,{|}_{z=g(x,y)} & =⟨2xy,\\,2z-{y}^{2},\\,2y⟩{|}_{z=4-x-y} \\\\  & =⟨2xy,\\,2(4-x-y)-{y}^{2},\\,2y⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{{2xy}_{⏟}}_{P},\\,{{8-2x-2y-{y}^{2}}_{⏟}}_{Q},\\,{{2y}_{⏟}}_{R}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{T}}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-(2xy)\\cdot (-1)-(8-2x-2y-{y}^{2})\\cdot (-1)+2y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(2xy-2x-{y}^{2}+8)\\,\\text{d}A,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is region enclosed by the projection of $C$ onto the $xy$-plane, shown below.\n\n![](images/d0/d0f9cb64d0ae1c3615df5a05c9f580bb9c74f6529efd732cfd83e1f4328ff8a3.png)\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}(2xy-2x-{y}^{2}+8)\\,\\text{d}A={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}2xy-2x-{y}^{2}+8\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281453 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0f463057-f805-5d6c-b391-8d33bfb0e1ec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1-x}\\,{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278729} {:answer/id #uuid "6e3f0c4c-50ad-5a82-9743-532f6b798a3b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{x-1}\\,-2xy+1\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279595} {:answer/id #uuid "77ebb165-94ff-572d-849d-60ea7ccc9364" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{x-1}\\,2xy+1\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279662} {:answer/id #uuid "ba62a950-4c28-54b0-850e-c0240391ff63" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{x-1}^{0}\\,2xy+1\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280236} {:answer/id #uuid "dff98bc2-4344-5634-a1af-59494d77062c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{x-1}^{0}\\,-2xy-1\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280569}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ba62a950-4c28-54b0-850e-c0240391ff63" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{x-1}^{0}\\,2xy+1\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280236} :answer-field/id #uuid "010aaa59-5dc6-5e8e-a83f-1b98c7c0cffc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280837}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "240db9c9-76c2-5c97-8e69-9ca188d1d1d5" :question/math-academy-id "q-162361" :question/problem "Let $C$ be the intersection of the paraboloid $z=1+{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}$ and the boundary of the infinite vertical column enclosed by the planes $x-y=1,$ $x=0,$ and $y=0.$ Given that the curve $C$ is oriented counter-clockwise when viewed from above and $\\text{curl}\\,F=⟨y,\\,-x,\\,1⟩,$ the circulation of $F$ along $C$ can be written as \n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by a simple, closed, piecewise-smooth boundary $C$ with positive orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $S,$ then Stokes' theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nThere are many surfaces whose boundary is the curve $C$ representing the intersection of the paraboloid $z=1+{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}$ and the boundary of our infinite vertical column. One such surface is our paraboloid $S$ with the equation \n\n$$\nz=g(x,y)=1+{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the partial derivatives of the graph $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(1+{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2})=2x \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(1+{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2})=4y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the equation $z=g(x,y)$ into expression for $\\text{curl}\\,F:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{curl}\\,F\\,{|}_{z=g(x,y)} & =⟨y,\\,-x,\\,1⟩{|}_{z=1+{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =⟨y,\\,-x,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{{y}_{⏟}}_{P},\\,{{-x}_{⏟}}_{Q},\\,{{1}_{⏟}}_{R}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-y\\cdot (2x)-(-x)\\cdot (4y)+1)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(2xy+1)\\,\\text{d}A,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is region enclosed by the projection of $C$ onto the $xy$-plane, shown below.\n\n![](images/d8/d84b3ecc79c58a128d2f7ea262692d9f66dd9882ff244fb7c3dadd2b2571b550.png)\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}(2xy+1)\\,\\text{d}A & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{x-1}^{0}\\,2xy+1\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281454 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0db0748b-118a-5639-b8f8-101df0972775" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\pi {e}^{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278719} {:answer/id #uuid "3d2fab1f-e285-51f4-a3b5-fff44aa9419e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{\\pi }^{2}+{e}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279130} {:answer/id #uuid "7b0afc91-4186-596b-a560-07301b9b3b9b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi +{e}^{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279689} {:answer/id #uuid "820edc0c-36f3-5234-a0a6-bb7079f70fad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\pi +{e}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279757} {:answer/id #uuid "8f71466e-d558-54ce-b28e-4bc0ccfab292" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {e}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279866}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0db0748b-118a-5639-b8f8-101df0972775" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\pi {e}^{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278719} :answer-field/id #uuid "2cbd89c4-cb59-5b1c-a88e-c10bdb94a980" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280915}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "ae18124e-1564-593a-8b3e-a861867113b8" :question/math-academy-id "q-162366" :question/problem "Use Stokes' theorem to evaluate \n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\text{curl}\\,F=⟨-x{e}^{z},\\,0,\\,{e}^{z}⟩,$ and $C$ is the intersection of the paraboloid $z={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}$ and the plane $z=4.$ The curve $C$ is oriented counter-clockwise when viewed from above." :question/worked-solution "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by a simple, closed, piecewise-smooth boundary $C$ with positive orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $S,$ then Stokes' theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nThere are many surfaces whose boundary is the curve $C$ representing the intersection of the paraboloid $z={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}$ and the plane $z=4.$ One such surface would be the plane $S$ with the equation \n\n$$\nz=g(x,y)=4.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the partial derivatives of the graph $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4)=0 \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(4)=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the equation $z=g(x,y)$ into the expression for $\\text{curl}\\,F:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{curl}\\,F\\,{|}_{z=g(x,y)} & =⟨-x{e}^{z},\\,0,\\,{e}^{z}⟩{|}_{z=4} \\\\  & =⟨{{-x{e}^{4}}_{⏟}}_{P},\\,{{0}_{⏟}}_{Q},\\,{{{e}^{4}}_{⏟}}_{R}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-0\\cdot (-x{e}^{4})-0\\cdot 0+{e}^{4})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}{e}^{4}\\,\\text{d}A,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is region enclosed by the projection of $C$ onto the $xy$-plane.\n\nNotice that the projection of $C$ onto the $xy$-plane is the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4$ with radius $2$ centered at the origin. Therefore, using the change of variables to polar coordinates\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\text{d}A=r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta ,\n$$\n\n \n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}{e}^{4}\\,\\text{d}A & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2}{e}^{4}\\cdot r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={e}^{4}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{2}r\\,\\text{d}r \\\\  & ={e}^{4}[\\theta ]_{\\theta =0}^{\\theta =2\\pi }\\cdot {[\\frac{{r}^{2}}{2}]}_{r=0}^{r=2} \\\\  & ={e}^{4}(2\\pi -0)\\cdot (2-0) \\\\  & =4\\pi {e}^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281457 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5032ce38-7144-56f3-8abf-062a36b12bf4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279325} {:answer/id #uuid "83b488ab-928c-5811-9c80-8d860a67a666" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279771} {:answer/id #uuid "9b85bad0-7c0c-5cc8-b1a9-087dcba76ead" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279970} {:answer/id #uuid "e475d0cc-7991-51cb-aee6-efc92dbc2dff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280609} {:answer/id #uuid "fb7ed414-d61d-5709-ad35-0b55e3209b8c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280795}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e475d0cc-7991-51cb-aee6-efc92dbc2dff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280609} :answer-field/id #uuid "7e171a0c-c922-554a-8200-5911711795ea" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281065}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "db5de703-3efe-5274-901b-09592c12a8ca" :question/math-academy-id "q-162374" :question/problem "Use Stokes' theorem to evaluate \n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\text{curl}\\,F=⟨0,\\,z,\\,x⟩,$ and $C$  is the intersection of the cylinder ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1$ and the plane $z=x.$ The curve $C$ is oriented counter-clockwise when viewed from above." :question/worked-solution "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by a simple, closed, piecewise-smooth boundary $C$ with positive orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $S,$ then Stokes' theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nThere are many surfaces whose boundary is the curve $C$ representing the intersection of the cylinder ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1$ and the plane $z=x.$ One such surface would be the plane $S$ with the equation \n\n$$\nz=g(x,y)=x.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the partial derivatives of the graph $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x)=1 \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x)=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the equation $z=g(x,y)$ into the expression for $\\text{curl}\\,F:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{curl}\\,F\\,{|}_{z=g(x,y)} & =⟨0,\\,z,\\,x⟩{|}_{z=x} \\\\  & =⟨{{0}_{⏟}}_{P},\\,{{x}_{⏟}}_{Q},\\,{{x}_{⏟}}_{R}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-0\\cdot 1-x\\cdot 0+x))\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}x\\,\\text{d}A,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is region enclosed by the projection of $C$ onto the $xy$-plane.\n\nNotice that the projection of $C$ onto the $xy$-plane is the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1$ with radius $1$ centered at the origin. Therefore, using the change of variables to polar coordinates\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\text{d}A=r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta ,\n$$\n\n \n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}x\\,\\text{d}A & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot r\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,\\text{d}r \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,\\text{d}r \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{[{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ]}_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}r \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372020 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3886a830-f807-532a-8dff-ac10e7ec4914" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-72\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372022} {:answer/id #uuid "7607b49a-d23b-55b8-8e3c-b7353e88eeeb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "144\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372023} {:answer/id #uuid "c46e2782-3e03-5ada-93b7-ba596554275e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-36\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372024} {:answer/id #uuid "0f26e94b-0e7f-527e-a515-9f633c3e5b33" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372025} {:answer/id #uuid "5c50dc6b-1088-5cb6-baa6-a6a933d4f39e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372026}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3886a830-f807-532a-8dff-ac10e7ec4914" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-72\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372022} :answer-field/id #uuid "10810889-3a2d-5477-b575-bc6677b334ca" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372021}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "da6c973e-9e1d-506f-8988-7e41ce9a8511" :question/math-academy-id "q-162371" :question/problem "Use Stokes' theorem to evaluate \n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\text{curl}\\,F=⟨y,\\,2yz,\\,-{z}^{2}+1⟩,$ and $C$ is the intersection of the half-cone $z=-\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}$ and the plane $z=-3.$ The curve $C$ is oriented counter-clockwise when viewed from above." :question/worked-solution "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by a simple, closed, piecewise-smooth boundary $C$ with positive orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $S,$ then Stokes' theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nThere are many surfaces whose boundary is the curve $C$ representing the intersection of the half-cone $z=-\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}$ and the plane $z=-3.$ One such surface would be the plane $S$ with the equation \n\n$$\nz=g(x,y)=-3.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the partial derivatives of the graph $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-3)=0 \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-3)=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the equation $z=g(x,y)$ into the expression for $\\text{curl}\\,F:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{curl}\\,F\\,{|}_{z=g(x,y)} & =⟨y,\\,2yz,\\,-{z}^{2}+1⟩{|}_{z=-3} \\\\  & =⟨{{y}_{⏟}}_{P},\\,{{-6y}_{⏟}}_{Q},\\,{{-8}_{⏟}}_{R}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-y\\cdot 0-(-6y)\\cdot 0-8)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}-8\\,\\text{d}A,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is region enclosed by the projection of $C$ onto the $xy$-plane.\n\nNotice that the projection of $C$ onto the $xy$-plane is the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=9$ with radius $3$ centered at the origin. Therefore, using the change of variables to polar coordinates\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\text{d}A=r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta ,\n$$\n\n \n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}-8\\,\\text{d}A & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{3}-8\\cdot r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =-8{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{3}r\\,\\text{d}r \\\\  & =-8[\\theta ]_{\\theta =0}^{\\theta =2\\pi }\\cdot {[\\frac{{r}^{2}}{2}]}_{r=0}^{r=3} \\\\  & =-8(2\\pi -0)\\cdot (\\frac{9}{2}-0) \\\\  & =-72\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372027 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ff3a9c27-f34b-5594-94af-40a7c58297f3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372029} {:answer/id #uuid "b5a89c10-18a7-59cb-8a1b-3a18b2a90938" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372030} {:answer/id #uuid "f2ff01ff-40d6-5616-bd3a-9376f9415536" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372031} {:answer/id #uuid "14cd568a-621b-5de0-baca-80df932e47d7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372032} {:answer/id #uuid "daa9659f-982a-5882-a68f-e91cd62724ff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372033}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "14cd568a-621b-5de0-baca-80df932e47d7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372032} :answer-field/id #uuid "5674f149-9ce3-5602-89ab-1339d40392d5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372028}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "00a16c1c-6465-5edc-becb-61f3932da901" :question/math-academy-id "q-162372" :question/problem "Use Stokes' theorem to evaluate \n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\text{curl}\\,F=⟨-y,\\,z,\\,-2x⟩,$ and $C$ is the intersection of the cylinder ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1$ and the plane $z=2+2x-y.$ The curve $C$ is oriented counter-clockwise when viewed from above." :question/worked-solution "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by a simple, closed, piecewise-smooth boundary $C$ with positive orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $S,$ then Stokes' theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nThere are many surfaces whose boundary is the curve $C$ representing the intersection of the cylinder ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1$ and the plane $z=2+2x-y.$ One such surface would be the plane $S$ with the equation \n\n$$\nz=g(x,y)=2+2x-y.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the partial derivatives of the graph $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2+2x-y)=2 \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2+2x-y)=-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the equation $z=g(x,y)$ into the expression for $\\text{curl}\\,F:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{curl}\\,F\\,{|}_{z=g(x,y)} & =⟨-y,\\,z,\\,-2x⟩{|}_{z=2+2x-y} \\\\  & =⟨{{-y}_{⏟}}_{P},\\,{{2+2x-y}_{⏟}}_{Q},\\,{{-2x}_{⏟}}_{R}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-(-y)\\cdot (2)-(2+2x-y)\\cdot (-1)-2x)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(2y+2+2x-y-2x)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(2+y)\\,\\text{d}A,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is region enclosed by the projection of $C$ onto the $xy$-plane.\n\nNotice that the projection of $C$ onto the $xy$-plane is the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1$ with radius $1$ centered at the origin. Therefore, using the change of variables to polar coordinates\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\text{d}A=r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta ,\n$$\n\n \n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}(2+y)\\,\\text{d}A & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{1}(2+r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\cdot r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{1}2r+{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{[{r}^{2}+\\frac{{r}^{3}}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ]}_{0}^{1}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(1+\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={[\\theta -\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ]}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =2\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401336 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "69eaa3fe-4c03-532c-9d1a-cd84b8703ad2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\,-\\frac{4}{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401338} {:answer/id #uuid "460112aa-e36b-52a6-a02b-783fee9d05b2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\,-\\frac{xy}{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401339} {:answer/id #uuid "9d2fbcee-821a-5cd9-8a6e-f9412bd4ab5e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\,\\frac{{x}^{2}}{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401340} {:answer/id #uuid "e12cffd5-0b22-5b0b-a3e3-7270fff61900" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\,-\\frac{2}{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401341} {:answer/id #uuid "1540b931-3add-5e5c-99b4-77aa9c86e5f9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\,-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401342}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e12cffd5-0b22-5b0b-a3e3-7270fff61900" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\,-\\frac{2}{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401341} :answer-field/id #uuid "17de6bba-7478-572d-9fe3-ada91a6e9edc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401337}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a28026ac-9427-5db7-b40f-31513274adc4" :question/math-academy-id "q-162365" :question/problem "Let $C$ be the intersection of the surface $z=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1}$ and the boundary of the infinite vertical column enclosed by the planes $x=\\pm 1,$ and $y=\\pm 1.$ Given that the curve $C$ is oriented counter-clockwise when viewed from above and $\\text{curl}\\,F=⟨x,\\,y,\\,-2z⟩,$ the circulation of $F$ along $C$ can be written as \n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by a simple, closed, piecewise-smooth boundary $C$ with positive orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $S,$ then Stokes' theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nThere are many surfaces whose boundary is the curve $C$ representing the intersection of the surface $z=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1}$ and the boundary of our infinite vertical column. One such surface is our surface $S$ with the equation \n\n$$\nz=g(x,y)=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1}.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the partial derivatives of the graph $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1})=\\frac{-2x}{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1)^{2}} \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1})=\\frac{-2y}{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1)^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the equation $z=g(x,y)$ into expression for $\\text{curl}\\,F:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{curl}\\,F\\,{|}_{z=g(x,y)} & =⟨x,\\,y,\\,-2z⟩{|}_{z=1/({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1)} \\\\  & =⟨{{x}_{⏟}}_{P},\\,{{y}_{⏟}}_{Q},\\,{{-\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1}}_{⏟}}_{R}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-x\\cdot \\frac{-2x}{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1)^{2}}-y\\cdot \\frac{-2y}{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1)^{2}}-\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}-\\frac{2}{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is region enclosed by the projection of $C$ onto the $xy$-plane, shown below.\n\n![](images/b2/b215ec1e3330f96e7e606e085721b2dd1f50d113f0151e36ec98e6d0a6f54e73.png)\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}-\\frac{2}{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\,-\\frac{2}{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186431184 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "06f86a33-c575-56fe-bdb7-9730885ac9ec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431147} {:answer/id #uuid "20c46104-aaee-5f81-87c8-e9c611ae6afd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431151} {:answer/id #uuid "54da2db0-1ce1-5f93-af27-c7663a8c8354" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431155} {:answer/id #uuid "c8614eac-4ce9-50b5-a743-5b04a0329efb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431170} {:answer/id #uuid "fe3f8a13-dd60-5d9d-81be-3575b6037de6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431176}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "20c46104-aaee-5f81-87c8-e9c611ae6afd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431151} :answer-field/id #uuid "257fc3b0-4eee-568e-ab48-ba72ee13cf19" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431177}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "4f7d4767-ad54-585a-81f3-9a4a8f681379" :question/math-academy-id "q-162373" :question/problem "Use Stokes' theorem to evaluate \n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\text{curl}\\,F=⟨x+y,\\,-x+y,\\,-2z⟩,$ and $C$ is the intersection of the cylinder ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1$ and the paraboloid $z=(x-1)^{2}+(y+1)^{2}.$ The curve $C$ is oriented counter-clockwise when viewed from above." :question/worked-solution "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by a simple, closed, piecewise-smooth boundary $C$ with positive orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $S,$ then Stokes' theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nThere are many surfaces whose boundary is the curve $C$ representing the intersection of the cylinder ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1$ and the paraboloid $z=(x-1)^{2}+(y+1)^{2}.$ One such surface would be the plane $S$ with the equation \n\n$$\nz=g(x,y)=(x-1)^{2}+(y+1)^{2}.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the partial derivatives of the graph $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}((x-1)^{2}+(y+1)^{2})=2(x-1) \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}((x-1)^{2}+(y+1)^{2})=2(y+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the equation $z=g(x,y)$ into the expression for $\\text{curl}\\,F:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{curl}\\,F\\,{|}_{z=g(x,y)} & =⟨x+y,\\,-x+y,\\,-2z⟩{|}_{z=(x-1)^{2}+(y+1)^{2}} \\\\  & =⟨{{x+y}_{⏟}}_{P},\\,{{-x+y}_{⏟}}_{Q},\\,{{-2(x-1)^{2}-2(y+1)^{2}}_{⏟}}_{R}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-(x+y)\\cdot 2(x-1)-(-x+y)\\cdot 2(y+1)-2((x-1)^{2}+(y+1)^{2}))\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2((x+y)(1-x)+(x-y)(y+1)-(x-1)^{2}-(y+1)^{2})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}4(-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}+2x-y-1)\\,\\text{d}A,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is region enclosed by the projection of $C$ onto the $xy$-plane.\n\nNotice that the projection of $C$ onto the $xy$-plane is the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1$ with radius $1$ centered at the origin. Therefore, using the change of variables to polar coordinates\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\text{d}A=r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta ,\n$$\n\n \n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}4(-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}+2x-y-1)\\,\\text{d}A & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }4(-{r}^{2}+2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -1)\\cdot r\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,\\text{d}r \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(-{r}^{3}+2{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -r)\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,\\text{d}r \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{0}^{1}{[-{r}^{3}\\theta +2{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -r\\theta ]}_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}r \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{0}^{1}(-2\\pi {r}^{3}-2\\pi r)\\,\\text{d}r \\\\  & =-8\\pi {[-\\frac{{r}^{4}}{4}-\\frac{{r}^{2}}{2}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =-8\\pi (-\\frac{1}{4}-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =-6\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186431187 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1ce41f48-3cd0-5535-a296-5747f3b20333" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{0}{\\int }_{0}^{1+x}\\,y({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431150} {:answer/id #uuid "3859679f-08da-5297-b35b-fcbd0703da61" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{0}{\\int }_{0}^{1+x}\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431153} {:answer/id #uuid "83932af9-e25e-5cce-b094-ca4fbc00e243" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{0}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\,y({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})^{3}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431158} {:answer/id #uuid "aeb9b526-feac-52a5-810d-3cdfe908fcdf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{0}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\,\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431166} {:answer/id #uuid "da95a228-ca5c-59a2-aafd-0aafd2625911" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{0}{\\int }_{0}^{1+x}\\,({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})^{2}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431172}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1ce41f48-3cd0-5535-a296-5747f3b20333" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{0}{\\int }_{0}^{1+x}\\,y({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431150} :answer-field/id #uuid "f3c5fce6-36d3-5f8a-948a-98bad40e6ffb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431182}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9aea1d19-f86f-5208-99cb-c76a6a49c45e" :question/math-academy-id "q-162364" :question/problem "Let $C$ be the intersection of the half-cone $z=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}$ and the boundary of the infinite vertical column enclosed by the planes $y=1+x,$ $x=0,$ and $y=0.$ Given that the curve $C$ is oriented counter-clockwise when viewed from above and $\\text{curl}\\,F=⟨0,\\,-{z}^{3},\\,0⟩,$ the circulation of $F$ along $C$ can be written as \n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by a simple, closed, piecewise-smooth boundary $C$ with positive orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $S,$ then Stokes' theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nThere are many surfaces whose boundary is the curve $C$ representing the intersection of the half-cone $z=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}$ and the boundary of our infinite vertical column. One such surface is our paraboloid $S$ with the equation \n\n$$\nz=g(x,y)=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the partial derivatives of the graph $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}=\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}} \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}=\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the equation $z=g(x,y)$ into expression for $\\text{curl}\\,F:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{curl}\\,F\\,{|}_{z=g(x,y)} & =⟨0\\,-{z}^{3},\\,0⟩{|}_{z=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}} \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})^{3/2},\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{{0}_{⏟}}_{P},\\,{{-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})^{3/2}}_{⏟}}_{Q},\\,{{0}_{⏟}}_{R}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-0\\cdot \\frac{x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}+({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})^{3/2}\\cdot \\frac{y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}+0)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}y({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is region enclosed by the projection of $C$ onto the $xy$-plane, shown below.\n\n![](images/ab/ab155520a21d38483e8b0978a04972e1a96c8d273d23ca7aa36bb1846189b4c8.png)\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}y({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}{\\int }_{0}^{1+x}\\,y({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Line Integral Using Stokes' Theorem"}]}
